eeds:\n\n- Evelyn: Distance = 4.5 units, Time = 1.25 units\n- Briana: Distance = 2.2 units, Time = 2.5 units\n- Carla: Distance = 5.2 units, Time = 4.25 units\n- Debra: Distance = 2.8 units, Time = 5.6 units\n- Angela: Distance = 1.4 units, Time = 6.8 units\n\nNow, let\'s calculate the average speeds using Python.\n```python\n# Distances and times for each student\ndistances = [4.5, 2.2, 5.2, 2.8, 1.4]\ntimes = [1.25, 2.5, 4.25, 5.6, 6.8]\n\n# Calculate average speeds\naverage_speeds = [dist / time for dist, time in zip(distances, times)]\n\n# Find the student with the greatest average speed\nmax_speed = max(average_speeds)\nindex_of_max_speed = average_speeds.index(max_speed)\n\n# List of students\nstudents = ["Evelyn", "Briana", "Carla", "Debra", "Angela"]\n\n# Find the student with the greatest average speed\nstudent_with_max_speed = students[index_of_max_speed]\nprint((student_with_max_speed, max_speed))\n```\n```output\n(\'Evelyn\', 3.6)\n```\nThe student with the greatest average speed is Evelyn, with an average speed of 3.6 units per time unit.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Evelyn}}\\).', "To determine which student has the greatest average speed, we need to calculate the slope of each student's distance-time graph. The slope of the graph represents the speed of the student. The greater the slope, the greater the speed.\n\nHere are the steps to solve the problem:\n\n1. Identify the coordinates of the points for each student.\n2. Calculate the slope for each student using the formula: \\(\\text{slope} = \\frac{\\Delta \\text{distance}}{\\Delta \\text{time}}\\).\n3. Compare the slopes to determine which student has the greatest average speed.\n\nLet's calculate the slopes for each student:\n\n- **Evelyn**: The coordinates are (1.25, 4.5). Assuming the starting point is (0, 0), the slope is \\(\\frac{4.5 - 0}{1.25 - 0} = \\frac{4.5}{1.25} = 3.6\\).\n- **Briana**: The coordinates are (2.5, 2.2). Assuming the starting point is (0, 0), the slope is