od of the sine wave is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{b} \\). From the graph, we can see that the period is \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), so \\( b = 3 \\).\n3. The vertical shift \\( d \\) is 1, as the midline of the sine wave is at \\( y = 1 \\).\n4. The phase shift \\( c \\) can be determined by looking at the horizontal shift of the graph. The standard sine function \\( \\sin(x) \\) starts at \\( y = 0 \\) when \\( x = 0 \\). In this graph, the sine wave starts at \\( y = -1 \\) when \\( x = 0 \\). This indicates a phase shift to the left by \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), so \\( c = -\\pi \\).\n\nHowever, we need to find the smallest possible positive value of \\( c \\). Since the sine function is periodic with a period of \\( 2\\pi \\), we can add \\( 2\\pi \\) to \\( -\\pi \\) to get a positive value. So, the smallest possible positive value of \\( c \\) is \\( \\pi \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n# Define the function\ndef f(x, a, b, c, d):\n    return a * np.sin(b * x + c) + d\n\n# Parameters\na = 2\nb = 3\nc = np.pi  # smallest positive phase shift\nd = 1\n\n# Generate x values\nx = np.linspace(-3 * np.pi, 3 * np.pi, 1000)\n\n# Compute y values\ny = f(x, a, b, c, d)\n\n# Plot the function\nplt.plot(x, y, label='y = 2 sin(3x + π) + 1')\nplt.xlabel('x')\nplt.ylabel('y')\nplt.title('Graph of y = 2 sin(3x + π) + 1')\nplt.legend()\nplt.grid(True)\nplt.show()\n\n# The smallest possible value of c\nsmallest_c = c\nprint(smallest_c)\n```\n```output\n3.141592653589793\n```\nThe smallest possible value of \\( c \\) for the function \\( y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d \\) is \\(\\pi\\), which is approximately 3.14159. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to identify the value of \\(c\\) in the function \\(y = a \\sin(bx + c) + d\\) given the graph. The value of \\(c\\) is related to the phase shift of the sine function. The phase shift of the sine 