, the constant term).\n\nThe general term in the expansion is given by:\n\n\\[\nT_k = \\binom{5}{k} (10x^3)^{5-k} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^k\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the general term, we get:\n\n\\[\nT_k = \\binom{5}{k} 10^{5-k} x^{3(5-k)} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k x^{-2k} = \\binom{5}{k} 10^{5-k} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k x^{15-3k-2k} = \\binom{5}{k} 10^{5-k} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k x^{15-5k}\n\\]\n\nFor the term to be constant, the exponent of \\(x\\) must be zero. So, we set \\(15 - 5k = 0\\) and solve for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n15 - 5k = 0 \\implies k = 3\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(k = 3\\) into the general term to find the constant term:\n\n\\[\nT_3 = \\binom{5}{3} 10^{5-3} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = \\binom{5}{3} 10^2 \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) = 10 \\cdot 100 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) = -\\frac{1000}{8} = -125\n\\]\n\nSo, the constant term in the expansion of \\(\\left(10x^3 - \\frac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^5\\) is \\(-125\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\n# Given values\nn = 5\na = 10\nb = -1/2\n\n# Finding the constant term\nk = 3\nconstant_term = comb(n, k) * (a**(n-k)) * (b**k)\nprint(constant_term)\n```\n```output\n-125.0\n```\nThe constant term in the expansion of \\(\\left(10x^3 - \\frac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^5\\) is \\(\\boxed{-125}\\).", "To find the constant term in the expansion of \\(\\left(10x^3 - \\frac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^5\\), we need to use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(a = 10x^3\\), \\(b = -\\frac{1}{2x^2}\\), and \\(n = 5\\). We need to find the term in the expansion where the exponent of \\(x\\) is zero (i.e., the constant term).\n\nThe general term in the expansion is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{k} (10x^3)^{5-k} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2x^2}\\right)^k\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the general term, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{k} 10^{5-k} x^{3(5-k)} \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^k 