 the roots explicitly. The 12th roots of unity are the solutions to the equation \\( z^{12} - 1 = 0 \\). The roots are \\( e^{2k\\pi i / 12} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 11 \\). The roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are:\n\\[ z = e^{2\\pi i / 12}, e^{10\\pi i / 12}, e^{14\\pi i / 12}, e^{22\\pi i / 12}. \\]\nThese are indeed 12th roots of unity.\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nz = sp.symbols('z')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial\r\npolynomial = z**4 + z**2 + 1\r\n\r\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\r\nroots = sp.solve(polynomial, z)\r\n\r\n# Check if these roots are nth roots of unity for some n\r\ndef is_nth_root_of_unity(root, n):\r\n    return sp.simplify(root**n - 1) == 0\r\n\r\n# Find the smallest n such that all roots are nth roots of unity\r\nn = 1\r\nwhile True:\r\n    if all(is_nth_root_of_unity(root, n) for root in roots):\r\n        break\r\n    n += 1\r\n\r\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that the code took too long to execute. Let's simplify the approach by directly checking the roots and their properties without iterating indefinitely. We know from our earlier reasoning that the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are the 12th roots of unity. We can confirm this by checking the properties of the roots directly.\n\nLet's verify that the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are indeed the 12th roots of unity by checking if they satisfy \\( z^{12} = 1 \\).\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the roots of the polynomial z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\r\nroots = [cmath.exp(2j * cmath.pi * k / 12) for k in [1, 5, 7, 11]]\r\n\r\n# Check if these roots are 12th roots of unity\r\ndef is_12th_root_of_unity(root):\r\n    return abs(r