 one of the activities is \\(50 - N = 50 - 6 = 44\\). Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion, we have:\n\\[M + S - B = 44\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[28 + 21 - B = 44\\]\n\\[49 - B = 44\\]\n\\[B = 49 - 44 = 5\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. The principle of inclusion and exclusion for two sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n|A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B|\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(|A \\cup B|\\) is the number of students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both, \\(|A|\\) is the number of students who participate in MATHCOUNTS, \\(|B|\\) is the number of students who participate in science club, and \\(|A \\cap B|\\) is the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and science club.\n\nGiven:\n- Total number of students, \\(N = 50\\)\n- Number of students who participate in MATHCOUNTS, \\(|A| = 28\\)\n- Number of students who participate in science club, \\(|B| = 21\\)\n- Number of students who participate in neither, \\(N - |A \\cup B| = 6\\)\n\nFirst, we can find the number of students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both:\n\n\\[\n|A \\cup B| = N - 6 = 50 - 6 = 44\n\\]\n\nNow, using the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we can solve for \\(|A \\cap B|\\):\n\n\\[\n|A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[\n44 = 28 + 21 - |A \\cap B|\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(|A \\cap B|\\):\n\n\\[\n|A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 = 5\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is 5. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nmathcounts_students = 28\nscience_club_students = 21\nneither_students = 6\n\n# Students who participate in either MATHCOUNTS or science club or both\neither_or_both_students = total_students - neither_students\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students who participate in both\nboth_students = mathcounts_students + science_club_students - either_or_both_students\nprint(both_students)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. The principle of inclusion and exclusion for two sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ |A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B| \\]\n\nHere, \\(A\\) represents the set of students participating in MATHCOUNTS, and \\(B\\) represents the set of students participating in the science club. The total number of students in the class is 50, and 6 students do not participate in either activity. Therefore, the number of students participating in at least one of the activities is \\(50 - 6 = 44\\). This is the value of \\( |A \\cup B| \\).\n\nWe know that \\( |A| = 28 \\) and \\( |B| = 21 \\). We need to find \\( |A \\cap B| \\), the number of students participating in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club. Rearranging the inclusion-exclusion formula, we get:\n\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cup B| \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[ |A \\cap B| = 28 + 21 - 44 \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\ntotal_students = 50\nstudents_neither = 6\nstudents_mathcounts = 28\nstudents_science_club = 21\n\n# Students participating in at least one of the activities\nstudents_at_least_one_activity = total_students - students_neither\n\n# Using the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students in both activities\nstudents_both_activities = students_mathcounts + students_science_club - students_at_least_one_activity\nprint(students_both_activities)\n```\n```output\n5\n```\nThe number of students who participate in both MATHCOUNTS and the science club is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", "To determine how 