oots $a, b, c$ of the polynomial $x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0$. We can use numerical methods to find the roots, but for simplicity, let\'s assume we have the roots and proceed with the analysis.\n\nGiven the polynomial $x^3 - 3x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0$, we know:\n\\[ a + b + c = 3 \\]\n\\[ ab + bc + ca = 4 \\]\n\\[ abc = 1 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the polynomial whose roots are $a^3, b^3, c^3$. Using the identity for the cube of the roots, we have:\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3abc = (a+b+c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - bc - ca) \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3 \\cdot 1 = 3((a+b+c)^2 - 3(ab + bc + ca)) \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3 = 3(3^2 - 3 \\cdot 4) \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3 = 3(9 - 12) \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3 = 3(-3) \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3 = -9 \\]\n\\[ a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = -6 \\]\n\nSimilarly, we can find the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time:\n\\[ a^3b^3 + b^3c^3 + c^3a^3 = (ab + bc + ca)^3 - 3abc(a^3 + b^3 + c^3) - 3(abc)^2 \\]\n\\[ a^3b^3 + b^3c^3 + c^3a^3 = 4^3 - 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-6) - 3 \\cdot 1^2 \\]\n\\[ a^3b^3 + b^3c^3 + c^3a^3 = 64 + 18 - 3 \\]\n\\[ a^3b^3 + b^3c^3 + c^3a^3 = 79 \\]\n\nAnd the product of the roots:\n\\[ a^3b^3c^3 = (abc)^3 = 1^3 = 1 \\]\n\nSo the polynomial whose roots are $a^3, b^3, c^3$ is:\n\\[ t^3 + 6t^2 + 79t - 1 = 0 \\]\n\nSince the polynomial $x^9 + px^6 + qx^3 + r$ has roots $a^3, b^3, c^3$, we can write:\n\\[ x^9 + px^6 + qx^3 + r = (x^3)^3 + 6(x^3)^2 + 79(x^3) - 1 \\]\n\\[ x^9 + px^6 + qx^3 + r = x^9 + 6x^6 + 79x^3 - 1 \\]\n\nThus, the ordered triple $(p, q, r)$ is $(6, 79, -1)$. Let\'s confirm this with Python code.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the polynomial and its roots\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\npoly = x**3 - 3*x**2 + 4*x - 1\nroots = sp.solve(poly, x)\n\n# Calculate the new roots which are the cubes of the original roots\nnew_roots = [root**3 for root in roots]\n\n# Find the polynomial with the new roots\nnew_poly = sp.poly_from_roots(new_roots, x)[0]\n\n# Extract the coefficients\ncoeffs = sp.Poly(n