]\nSubtract \\(x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n4x - 1 = 3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\nDivide by 4:\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\\[\n5x - 1 = -x - 3\n\\]\nAdd \\(x\\) to both sides:\n\\[\n6x - 1 = -3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n6x = -2\n\\]\nDivide by 6:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two potential solutions: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). To find the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the original equation, we need to check which of these solutions is larger. Clearly, \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nThus, the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\ndef check_solution(x):\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\n\n# Potential solutions\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\n\n# Check which solution satisfies the equation\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\n\n# Find the largest valid solution\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\), we need to consider the two possible cases for the absolute value expression:\n\n1. \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n2. \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n### Case 1: \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n\n\\[5x - 1 = x + 3\\]\n\\[5x - x = 3 + 1\\]\n\\[4x = 4\\]\n\\[x = 1\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\n\\[5x - 1 = -x - 3\\]\n\\[5x + x = -3 + 1\\]\n\\[6x = -2\\]\n\\[x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\]\n\nWe have two potential solutions: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). To determine which is the largest, we compare the two values. Clearly, \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\), so the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is \\(1\\).\n\nLet's verify that both solutions satisfy the