ion \\( |5x - 1| = x + 3 \\).\n\n### Verification\n\n1. For \\( x = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n|5(1) - 1| = 1 + 3 \\implies |4| = 4 \\implies 4 = 4\n\\]\n\nThis is true, so \\( x = 1 \\) is a valid solution.\n\n2. For \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\):\n\n\\[\n|5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3 \\implies \\left|-\\frac{5}{3} - 1\\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\left|-\\frac{8}{3}\\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis is true, so \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\) is also a valid solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation, we compare the two solutions \\( x = 1 \\) and \\( x = -\\frac{1}{3} \\). Clearly, \\( 1 \\) is greater than \\( -\\frac{1}{3} \\).\n\nThus, the largest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation is \\( x = 1 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]', "To solve the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\), we need to consider the two cases that arise from the absolute value:\n\n1. \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n2. \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n### Case 1: \\(5x - 1 = x + 3\\)\n\\[\n5x - 1 = x + 3\n\\]\nSubtract \\(x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n4x - 1 = 3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n4x = 4\n\\]\nDivide by 4:\n\\[\nx = 1\n\\]\n\n### Case 2: \\(5x - 1 = -(x + 3)\\)\n\\[\n5x - 1 = -x - 3\n\\]\nAdd \\(x\\) to both sides:\n\\[\n6x - 1 = -3\n\\]\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\\[\n6x = -2\n\\]\nDivide by 6:\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow we have two potential solutions: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\). To find the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation, we compare these two solutions. Clearly, \\(1 > -\\frac{1}{3}\\), so the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Potential solutions\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# V