ues of $\\lambda$ that satisfy it while ensuring $f(x) \\neq x$.\n\nThe equation we derived earlier was:\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 (1 - x)(1 - \\lambda x + \\lambda x^2) = 1\n\\]\nThis equation must hold for some $x \\in [0,1]$ such that $f(x) \\neq x$. Let's analyze this equation further.\n\nFor $x = 0$ or $x = 1$, we have $f(x) = 0$ or $f(x) = 0$, respectively, which means $f(x) = x$. So, we need to consider $x \\in (0,1)$. For $x \\in (0,1)$, we need to check if there are any values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation while ensuring $f(x) \\neq x$.\n\nLet's recheck the equation for specific values of $\\lambda$ manually. For example, let's consider $\\lambda = 2$:\n\\[\n2^2 (1 - x)(1 - 2x + 2x^2) = 1 \\implies 4 (1 - x)(1 - 2x + 2x^2) = 1\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n4 (1 - x)(1 - 2x + 2x^2) = 1 \\implies 4 (1 - 3x + 4x^2 - 2x^3) = 1 \\implies 4 - 12x + 16x^2 - 8x^3 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\implies 8x^3 - 16x^2 + 12x - 3 = 0\n\\]\nThis cubic equation has a root $x = \\frac{1}{2}$, but $f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}$, so $f(x) = x$.\n\nSimilarly, for $\\lambda = 3$:\n\\[\n3^2 (1 - x)(1 - 3x + 3x^2) = 1 \\implies 9 (1 - x)(1 - 3x + 3x^2) = 1\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n9 (1 - x)(1 - 3x + 3x^2) = 1 \\implies 9 (1 - 4x + 6x^2 - 3x^3) = 1 \\implies 9 - 36x + 54x^2 - 27x^3 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\implies 27x^3 - 54x^2 + 36x - 8 = 0\n\\]\nThis cubic equation has a root $x = \\frac{2}{3}$, but $f\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\left(1 - \\frac{2}{3}\\right) = \\frac{2}{3}$, so $f(x) = x$.\n\nFor $\\lambda = 4$:\n\\[\n4^2 (1 - x)(1 - 4x + 4x^2) = 1 \\implies 16 (1 - x)(1 - 4x + 4x^2) = 1\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n16 (1 - x)(1 - 4x + 4x^2) = 1 \\implies 16 (1 - 5x + 8x^2 - 4x^3) = 1 \\implies 16 - 80x + 128x^2 - 64x^3 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\implies 64x^3 - 128x^2 + 80x - 15 = 0\n\\]\nThis cubic equation has a root $x = \\frac{3}{4}$, but $f\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) = 4 \\cdot \\fr