- \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\cos 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\\).\n\n2. **Use Trigonometric Identities:**\n   We can use the product-to-sum identities and angle addition/subtraction formulas to simplify the expressions.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to find the simplified form and then determine the angle \\(\\theta\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangle_5 = sp.rad(5)\nangle_20 = sp.rad(20)\nangle_35 = sp.rad(35)\nangle_50 = sp.rad(50)\n\n# Define the expressions for the numerator and denominator\nnumerator = sp.cos(angle_5) * sp.cos(angle_20) + sp.cos(angle_35) * sp.cos(angle_50) - sp.sin(angle_5) * sp.sin(angle_20) - sp.sin(angle_35) * sp.sin(angle_50)\ndenominator = sp.sin(angle_5) * sp.cos(angle_20) - sp.sin(angle_35) * sp.cos(angle_50) + sp.cos(angle_5) * sp.sin(angle_20) - sp.cos(angle_35) * sp.sin(angle_50)\n\n# Simplify the expression for tan(theta)\ntan_theta = numerator / denominator\ntan_theta_simplified = sp.simplify(tan_theta)\n\n# Find the angle theta\ntheta = sp.atan(tan_theta_simplified)\ntheta_degrees = sp.deg(theta)\n\n# Ensure the angle is positive and within the least positive angle range\ntheta_degrees = sp.Mod(theta_degrees, 360)\n\nprint(theta_degrees)\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: 'Timed Out'\n```\nIt seems that the initial approach might be too computationally intensive. Let's simplify the problem manually before implementing it in Python.\n\n### Manual Simplification\n\nWe can use trigonometric identities to simplify the given expressions. Notice that the given expression resembles the angle addition/subtraction formulas for sine and cosine.\n\n1. **Numerator:**\n   \\[\n   \\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\cos 35^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ - \\sin 5^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ - \\sin 35^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\n   \\]\n   This can be rewritten using the angle addition formula:\n   \\[\n   \\cos 5^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ