 we consider $g(f(x))$. Since $g(x)$ is even, we have $g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x))$. Because $g(x)$ is even, $g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$. So, $g(f(-x)) = g(f(x))$.\n   - Now, we consider $f(g(f(x)))$. Since $f(x)$ is odd, we have $f(g(f(-x))) = f(g(f(x)))$. Because $g(f(x))$ is even, we can substitute $-x$ into $g(f(x))$ and get the same result.\n   - Continuing this pattern, we see that each layer of the function composition maintains the properties of being even or odd. Specifically, since the innermost function is odd and the outer functions alternate between odd and even, the overall function will be odd.\n\nLet\'s confirm this reasoning with a Python code snippet using sympy to symbolically verify the properties.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the symbols\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\n\n# Define the properties of odd and even functions\ndef is_odd(f):\n    return sp.simplify(f.subs(x, -x) + f) == 0\n\ndef is_even(f):\n    return sp.simplify(f.subs(x, -x) - f) == 0\n\n# Define the functions f and g\nf = sp.Function(\'f\')\ng = sp.Function(\'g\')\n\n# Define the composite function\ncomposite_function = f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))\n\n# Check if the composite function is odd\nis_odd_composite = is_odd(composite_function)\n\n# Check if the composite function is even\nis_even_composite = is_even(composite_function)\n\nprint("Is the composite function odd?", is_odd_composite)\nprint("Is the composite function even?", is_even_composite)\n```\n```output\nIs the composite function odd? False\nIs the composite function even? False\n```\nThe output indicates that the composite function $f(f(g(f(g(f(x))))))$ is neither even nor odd. This suggests that the function does not satisfy the properties of being purely even or purely odd.\n\nTo further confirm this, let\'s reason through the function composition step by step:\n\n1. The innermost function is $f(x)$, which is odd. So, $f(-x) = -f(x)$.\n2. The next function is $g(f(x))$. Since $g(x)$ is even, $g(f(-x)) = g(-f(x)) = g(f(x))$. So, $g(