ore than 64 terms while maintaining these conditions.\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for \\( n \\) is 64. The sum of all possible values of \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find the value of \\(n\\) given the following conditions:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i = 96, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 = 144, \\quad \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3 = 216.\n\\]\nWe will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and the power mean inequality to help us find the solution.\n\nFirst, let's consider the arithmetic mean (AM), quadratic mean (QM), and cubic mean (CM) of the sequence \\(a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n\\).\n\nThe arithmetic mean (AM) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{AM} = \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i}{n} = \\frac{96}{n}.\n\\]\n\nThe quadratic mean (QM) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{QM} = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2}{n}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{n}} = \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n\\]\n\nThe cubic mean (CM) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{CM} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^3}{n}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{216}{n}} = \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}}.\n\\]\n\nBy the power mean inequality, we have:\n\\[\n\\text{AM} \\leq \\text{QM} \\leq \\text{CM}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{96}{n} \\leq \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}} \\leq \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}}.\n\\]\nWe can solve these inequalities step by step.\n\nFirst, let's solve \\(\\frac{96}{n} \\leq \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{96}{n} \\leq \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}} \\implies 96 \\sqrt{n} \\leq 12n \\implies 8 \\sqrt{n} \\leq n \\implies 64n \\leq n^2 \\implies n^2 - 64n \\geq 0 \\implies n(n - 64) \\geq 0.\n\\]\nThis inequality is satisfied for \\(n \\geq 64\\) or \\(n \\leq 0\\). Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, we have \\(n \\geq 64\\).\n\nNext, let's solve \\(\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}} \\leq \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{n}} \\leq \\frac{6}{\\sqrt[3]{n}} \\implies 12 \\sqrt[3]{n} \\leq 6 \\sqrt{n} \\implies 2 \\sqrt[3]{n} \\leq \\sqrt{n} \\implies 8n \\leq n^{3/2} \\implies n^{3/2} - 8n \\geq 0 \\implies 