 her bag and puts it into Bob's bag. After Alice's move, Bob's bag will have 6 balls, and Alice's bag will have 4 balls.\n\n3. **Bob's Move:**\n   - Bob then randomly selects one ball from his bag (which now has 6 balls) and puts it into Alice's bag. After Bob's move, Alice's bag will have 5 balls, and Bob's bag will have 5 balls.\n\n4. **Final State:**\n   - We need to calculate the probability that after these moves, the contents of both bags are the same.\n\nTo find the probability, we need to consider all possible outcomes of Alice's and Bob's moves. Let's use Python to simulate this process and calculate the probability.\n\nHere's the Python code to solve the problem:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport math\n\n# Define the initial balls in each bag\ninitial_balls = ['B', 'G', 'O', 'R', 'V']\n\n# Generate all possible moves Alice can make\nalice_moves = list(itertools.permutations(initial_balls, 1))\n\n# Initialize a counter for successful outcomes\nsuccessful_outcomes = 0\n\n# Iterate through each possible move Alice can make\nfor alice_move in alice_moves:\n    # Alice's bag after her move\n    alice_bag_after_alice = list(initial_balls)\n    alice_bag_after_alice.remove(alice_move[0])\n    alice_bag_after_alice.append(alice_move[0])\n    \n    # Bob's bag after Alice's move\n    bob_bag_after_alice = list(initial_balls)\n    bob_bag_after_alice.append(alice_move[0])\n    \n    # Generate all possible moves Bob can make\n    bob_moves = list(itertools.permutations(bob_bag_after_alice, 1))\n    \n    # Iterate through each possible move Bob can make\n    for bob_move in bob_moves:\n        # Bob's bag after his move\n        bob_bag_after_bob = bob_bag_after_alice\n        bob_bag_after_bob.remove(bob_move[0])\n        bob_bag_after_bob.append(bob_move[0])\n        \n        # Alice's bag after Bob's move\n        alice_bag_after_bob = alice_bag_after_alice\n        alice_bag_after_bob.remove(bob_move[0])\n        alice_bag_after_bob.append(bob_move[0])\n  