boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed', "To solve the problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of books remaining on each shelf after two books are taken off each shelf.\n2. Calculate the total number of books remaining on all three shelves.\n3. Calculate the number of math books remaining on the middle shelf.\n4. Determine the fraction of the remaining books that are math books.\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step and use Python to ensure accuracy.\n\n1. The top shelf originally has 8 books, so after removing 2 books, it has \\(8 - 2 = 6\\) books.\n2. The middle shelf originally has 10 books, so after removing 2 books, it has \\(10 - 2 = 8\\) books.\n3. The bottom shelf originally has 6 books, so after removing 2 books, it has \\(6 - 2 = 4\\) books.\n4. The total number of books remaining on all three shelves is \\(6 + 8 + 4 = 18\\).\n5. The number of math books remaining on the middle shelf is \\(8\\) (since no math books were removed from the middle shelf).\n6. The fraction of the remaining books that are math books is \\(\\frac{8}{18}\\), which simplifies to \\(\\frac{4}{9}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Initial number of books on each shelf\ntop_shelf_initial = 8\nmiddle_shelf_initial = 10\nbottom_shelf_initial = 6\n\n# Number of books removed from each shelf\nbooks_removed_per_shelf = 2\n\n# Number of books remaining on each shelf after removal\ntop_shelf_remaining = top_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\nmiddle_shelf_remaining = middle_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\nbottom_shelf_remaining = bottom_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\n\n# Total number of books remaining on all three shelves\ntotal_books_remaining = top_shelf_remaining + middle_shelf_remaining + bottom_shelf_remaining\n\n# Number of math books remaining on the middle shelf\nmath_books_remaining = middle_shelf_remaining\n\n# Fraction of the remaining books that are math books\nfraction_math_books = math_books_remaining / total_books_remaining\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nsimplified_fraction = Fraction(math_books_remaining, total_books_remaining)\n\nprint(simplified_fraction)\n```\n```output\n4/9\n```\nThe fraction of the books remaining on the three shelves that are math books is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{9}}\\).", "A new high-speed train travels $20$ miles per hour faster than an older train. If the older train travels $25$ miles in the same amount of time it takes the new train to travel $45$ miles, then how fast does the new train travel? \\boxed{}. \\end{align*}\n\nAssistant: To solve the problem, we need to determine the speed of the new high-speed train. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. Let the speed of the older train be \\( v \\) miles per hour.\n2. Then the speed of the new train is \\( v + 20 \\) miles per hour.\n3. The time it takes for the older train to travel 25 miles is \\( \\frac{25}{v} \\) hours.\n4. The time it takes for the new train to travel 45 miles is \\( \\frac{45}{v + 20} \\) hours.\n5. Since these times are equal, we can set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{25}{v} = \\frac{45}{v + 20}\n   \\]\n6. Solving this equation for \\( v \\):\n   \\[\n   25(v + 20) = 45v\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   25v + 500 = 45v\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   500 = 20v\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   v = 25\n   \\]\n7. So the speed of the older train is 25 miles per hour, and the speed of the new train is \\( 25 + 20 = 45 \\) miles per hour.\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\n# Define the speed of the older train\nv = 25\n\n# Calculate the speed of the new train\nnew_train_speed = v + 20\n\n# Verify the times are equal\ntime_older_train = 25 / v\ntime_new_train = 45 / new_train_speed\n\n# Check if the times are equal\nprint((new_train_speed, time_older_train == time_new_train))\n```\n```output\n(45, True)\n```\nThe speed of the new train is \\( \\boxed{45} \\) miles per hour, and the times are indeed equal, confirming the accuracy of the solution.", 'There are 100 jelly beans in a glass jar. Mrs. Copper’s kindergarten class normally has 24 kids, but 2 children called in sick and stayed home that day. The class decides to throw the jelly beans into the jar to make up for missing class. Each of the 22 children in the class gets to throw 5 jelly beans in the jar. How many jelly beans are in the jar after the children finish throwing their jelly beans? \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\boxed{110} \\