inates are:\n- For $x = -4$, $y = (-4)^2 - 8 = 16 - 8 = 8$\n- For $x = 1$, $y = (1)^2 - 8 = 1 - 8 = -7$\n- For $x = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}$, $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right)^2 - 8 = \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{31}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right) - 8 = -7 - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}$\n- For $x = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}$, $y = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right)^2 - 8 = \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{31}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right) - 8 = -7 + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}$\n\nThe product of the $y$-coordinates is:\n\n\\[\n8 \\cdot (-7) \\cdot \\left(-7 - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-7 + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the product of the complex conjugates:\n\n\\[\n\\left(-7 - \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-7 + \\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right) = (-7)^2 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{31}i}{2}\\right)^2 = 49 - \\left(-\\frac{31}{4}\\right) = 49 + \\frac{31}{4} = \\frac{196 + 31}{4} = \\frac{227}{4}\n\\]\n\nSo the product of the $y$-coordinates is:\n\n\\[\n8 \\cdot (-7) \\cdot \\frac{227}{4} = -56 \\cdot \\frac{227}{4} = -14 \\cdot 227 = -3178\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the $y$-coordinates of all the distinct solutions $(x,y)$ is \\(\\boxed{-3178}\\).", "To find the product of the $y$-coordinates of all the distinct solutions $(x,y)$ for the two equations $y = x^2 - 8$ and $y^2 = -5x + 44$, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Substitute the expression for $y$ from the first equation into the second equation.\n2. Solve the resulting equation for $x$.\n3. Use the values of $x$ to find the corresponding $y$ values.\n4. Compute the product of these $y$ values.\n\nLet's start by substituting $y = x^2 - 8$ into $y^2 = -5x + 44$:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 - 8)^2 = -5x + 44\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\nx^4 - 16x^2 + 64 = -5x + 44\n\\]\n\nRearranging all terms to one side, we obtain:\n\n\\[\nx^4 - 16x^2 + 5x + 20 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the roots of this p