\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. Now, combine these results:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. To combine these fractions, find a common denominator. The least common multiple of 2 and 3 is 6, so we rewrite the fractions with a common denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{6} + \\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{6} = \\frac{9\\sqrt{2} + 8\\sqrt{3}}{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. The expression is now in the form $\\frac{a\\sqrt{2} + b\\sqrt{3}}{c}$ where $a = 9$, $b = 8$, and $c = 6$. Therefore, $a + b + c = 9 + 8 + 6 = 23$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\nfrom sympy import sqrt, Rational\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpr = sqrt(2) + Rational(1, sqrt(2)) + sqrt(3) + Rational(1, sqrt(3))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expr = expr.simplify()\r\n\r\n# Extract the coefficients a, b, and c\r\na = simplified_expr.as_coefficients_dict()[sqrt(2)]\r\nb = simplified_expr.as_coefficients_dict()[sqrt(3)]\r\nc = simplified_expr.q\r\n\r\n# Calculate a + b + c\r\nresult = a + b + c\r\nprint((simplified_expr, a, b, c, result))\n```\n```output\nTypeError: invalid input: sqrt(2)\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the way the expression was defined in the code. Let's redefine the expression properly and ensure we extract the coefficients correctly. We'll use the `sympy` library to handle the symbolic computation.\n\nHere's the corrected code:\n```python\nfrom sympy import sqrt, Rational\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpr = sqrt(2) + 1/sqrt(2) + sqrt(3) + 1/sqrt(3)\r\n\r\n# Simplify the expression\r\nsimplified_expr = expr.simplify()\r\n\r\n# Extract the coefficients a, b, and c\r\na = simplified_expr.as_coefficients_dict()[sqrt(2)]\r\nb = simplified_expr.as_coefficients_dict()[sqr