 d = i - i = 0, \\quad c + d = -1 - i.\n\\]\n\nAgain, since \\( a + c = 0 \\) and \\( b + d = 0 \\), the product \\( (a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d) \\) contains two factors of zero, making the entire product zero.\n\nHowever, we need to consider the possibility of other configurations that might yield a non-zero maximum value. Let's consider the general case where \\( a, b, c, \\) and \\( d \\) are distinct complex numbers on the unit circle such that \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\). We can use the fact that the product of the sums of the roots of a polynomial is related to the coefficients of the polynomial.\n\nGiven \\( a + b + c + d = 0 \\), we can consider the polynomial \\( P(z) = (z - a)(z - b)(z - c)(z - d) \\). The constant term of this polynomial is \\( abcd \\), and the coefficient of \\( z^3 \\) is \\( -(a + b + c + d) = 0 \\). The coefficient of \\( z^2 \\) is \\( ab + ac + ad + bc + bd + cd \\), and the coefficient of \\( z \\) is \\( -(abc + abd + acd + bcd) \\). The constant term is \\( abcd \\).\n\nThe product \\( (a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d) \\) is related to the discriminant of the polynomial \\( P(z) \\). The discriminant of a polynomial \\( P(z) = z^4 + pz^2 + qz + r \\) is given by a specific formula involving the coefficients. For our polynomial, the discriminant is related to the product of the differences of the roots, which is \\( (a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(b - c)(b - d)(c - d) \\).\n\nThe maximum value of \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) is achieved when the roots are symmetrically placed on the unit circle, which means that the polynomial has a specific form. For the case of a square inscribed in the unit circle, the maximum value is \\( 4 \\).\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) is \\( \\boxed{4} \\).", "Since $a, b, c, d$ are distinct complex numbers on the unit circle, we can represent them as $a = e^{i\\theta_1}$, $b = e^{i\\theta_2}$, $c = e^{i\\the