value of the expression:\n\n\\[\n|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(', "To solve this problem, we need to maximize the expression \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\) given that \\(|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1\\) and \\(a + b + c + d = 0\\).\n\nSince \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) are complex numbers on the unit circle, we can represent them as \\(a = e^{i\\theta_1}\\), \\(b = e^{i\\theta_2}\\), \\(c = e^{i\\theta_3}\\), and \\(d = e^{i\\theta_4}\\). The condition \\(a + b + c + d = 0\\) implies that the sum of these complex numbers is zero.\n\nThe expression we want to maximize is \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\). We can use the fact that the magnitude of a product is the product of the magnitudes, so we need to maximize the product of the magnitudes of the pairwise sums.\n\nLet's use Python and sympy to find the maximum value of this expression. We'll use the fact that the magnitudes of the complex numbers are 1 and that their sum is zero.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the complex numbers a, b, c, d on the unit circle\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols('a b c d', complex=True)\n\n# Define the condition a + b + c + d = 0\ncondition = sp.Eq(a + b + c + d, 0)\n\n# Define the expression to maximize\nexpression = sp.Abs((a + b) * (a + c) * (a + d) * (b + c) * (b + d) * (c + d))\n\n# Since |a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1, we can use the fact that a, b, c, d are on the unit circle\n# We can parameterize a, b, c, d as e^(i*theta1), e^(i*theta2), e^(i*theta3), e^(i*theta4)\ntheta1, theta2, theta3, theta4 = sp.symbols('theta1 theta2 theta3 theta4', real=True)\na = sp.exp(sp.I * theta1)\nb = sp.exp(sp.I * theta2)\nc = sp.exp(sp.I * theta3)\nd = sp.exp(sp.I * theta4)\n\n# Substitute the parameterized forms into the condition\ncondition_sub = condition.subs({a: sp.exp(sp.I * theta1), b: sp.exp(sp.I * theta2), c: sp.exp(sp.I * theta3), d: sp.exp(sp.I * theta4)})\n\n# Solve the condition for one of the angles in terms of the others\nsolution = sp.solve(condition_sub, theta4)\n\n# Substitute the solution back into the expression\nexpression_sub = expression.subs(d, sp.exp(sp.I * solution[0]))\n\n# Simplify the expression\nexpression_simplified = sp.simplify(expression_sub)\n\n# Find the maximum value of the expression\nmax_value = sp.Max(expression_simplified)\n\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\nAbs((a + b)*(a + c)*(a + d)*(b + c)*(b + d)*(c + d))\n```\nIt seems that the code did not simplify the expression as expected. Let's take a different approach to solve this problem. We'll use the fact that the complex numbers \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) are on the unit circle and their sum is zero. This means that they form a rectangle in the complex plane.\n\nGiven that \\(a + b + c + d = 0\\), we can assume without loss of generality that \\(a = e^{i\\theta}\\), \\(b = e^{-i\\theta}\\), \\(c = e^{i\\phi}\\), and \\(d = e^{-i\\phi}\\). This ensures that the sum of these complex numbers is zero.\n\nThe expression we want to maximize is \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\). Let's substitute the parameterized forms of \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) into this expression and simplify it.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles theta and phi\ntheta, phi = sp.symbols('theta phi', real=True)\n\n# Define the complex numbers a, b, c, d on the unit circle\na = sp.exp(sp.I * theta)\nb = sp.exp(-sp.I * theta)\nc = sp.exp(sp.I * phi)\nd = sp.exp(-sp.I * phi)\n\n# Define the expression to maximize\nexpression = sp.Abs((a + b) * (a + c) * (a + d) * (b + c) * (b + d) * (c + d))\n\n# Simplify the expression\nexpression_simplified = sp.simplify(expression)\n\n# Find the maximum value of the expression\nmax_value = sp.Max(expression_simplified)\n\nprint(max_value)\n```\n```output\n2*Abs((exp(2*I*phi) + 1)*(exp(2*I*theta) + 1))\n```\nThe output \\(2 \\cdot \\text{Abs}((\\exp(2i\\phi) + 1)(\\exp(2i\\theta) + 1))\\) can be further simplified. Since \\(\\exp(2i\\phi)\\) and \\(\\exp(2i\\theta)\\) are points on the unit circle, \\(\\exp(2i\\phi) + 1\\) and \\(\\exp(2i\\theta) + 1\\) are complex numbers with magnitudes equal to the distance from \\(-1\\) to the point on the unit circle. The maximum value of the magnitude of \\(\\exp(2i\\phi) + 1\\) or \\(\\exp(2i\\theta) + 1\\) is 2, which occurs when \\(\\phi\\) or \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\pi/2\\) or \\(-\\pi/2\\).\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of the expression is \\(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8\\).\n\nSo the maximum value of \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).", 'Since $|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1,$ we can write $a = e^{i\\theta_1},$ $b = e^{i\\theta_2},$ $c = e^{i\\theta_3},$ and $d = e^{i\\theta_4},$ where $\\theta_1,$ $\\theta_2,$ $\\theta_3,$ and $\\theta_4$ are real numbers.\n\nThen,\n\\begin{align*}\n|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| &= |(e^{i\\theta_1} + e^{i\\theta_2})(e^{i\\theta_1} + e^{i\\theta_3})(e^{i\\theta_1} + e^{i\\theta_4})(e^{i\\theta_2} + e^{i\\theta_3})(e^{i\\theta_2} + e^{i\\theta_4})(e^{i\\theta_3} + e^{i\\theta_4})| \\\\\n&= |(e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2)} + 1)(e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_3)} + 1)(e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_4)} + 1)(e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_3)} + 1)(e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_4)} + 1)(e^{i(\\theta_3 - \\theta_4)} + 1)|.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $a + b + c + d = 0,$ we have $e^{i\\theta_1} + e^{i\\theta_2} + e^{i\\theta_3} + e^{i\\theta_4} = 0.$  This implies that $e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2)} + e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_3)} + e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_4)} + e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_3)} + e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_4)} + e^{i(\\theta_3 - \\theta_4)} = -2.$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\\[|e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2)} + e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_3)} + e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_4)} + e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_3)} + e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_4)} + e^{i(\\theta_3 - \\theta_4)}| \\le |e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2)}| + |e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_3)}| + |e^{i(\\theta_1 - \\theta_4)}| + |e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_3)}| + |e^{i(\\theta_2 - \\theta_4)}| + |e^{i(\\theta_3 - \\theta_4)}| = 6.\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\le 6.\\]\n\nTo show that this maximum value is attainable, we can take $a = 1,$ $b = i,$ $c = -1,$ and $d = -i.$  Then,\n\\begin{align*}\n|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| &= |(1 + i)(1 - 1)(1 - i)(i - 1)(i + 1)(-1 - i)| \\\\\n&= |(1 + i)(0)(1 - i)(i - 1)(i + 1)(-1 - i)| \\\\\n&= 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|$ is $\\boxed{6}.$', "To find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to express \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) in terms of its columns. Let \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\mathbf{a}_1 & \\mathbf{a}_2 & \\mathbf{a}_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\) are the columns of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nFrom the given information, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = 3\\mathbf{a}_1 + \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = -5\\mathbf{a}_1 + 2\\mathbf{a}_2 + 2\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -13\\mathbf{a}_1 + 3\\mathbf{a}_2 + 4\\mathbf{a}_3\\).\n\nFirst, we solve for \\(\\mathbf{a}_1\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a}_2\\) from the first equation:\n\\[\n3\\mathbf{a}_1 + \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nLet's denote \\(\\mathbf{a}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ x_2 \\\\ x_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\\\ y_2 \\\\ y_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Then we have:\n\\[\n3\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ x_2 \\\\ x_3 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\\\ y_2 \\\\ y_3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n3x_1 + y_1 = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n3x_2 + y_2 = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n3x_3 + y_3 = -3\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the second equation to find \\(\\mathbf{a}_3\\):\n\\[\n-5\\mathbf{a}_1 + 2\\mathbf{a}_2 + 2\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(\\mathbf{a}_1\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a}_2\\) into this equation, we get:\n\\[\n-5\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ x_2 \\\\ x_3 \\end{pmatrix} + 2\\begin{pmatrix} y_1 \\\\ y_2 \\\\ y_3 \\end{pmatrix} + 2\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n-5x_1 + 2y_1 + 2a_{31} = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x_2 + 2y_2 + 2a_{32} = -5\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x_3 + 2y_3 + 2a_{33} = -5\n\\]\n\nWe can solve these equations step by step. First, let's solve for \\(\\mathbf{a}_1\\) and \\(\\mathbf{a}_2\\) from the first set of equations. We can express \\(\\mathbf{a}_2\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}_1\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 3x_1 \\\\ 4 - 3x_2 \\\\ -3 - 3x_3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(\\mathbf{a}_2\\) into the second set of equations to find \\(\\mathbf{a}_3\\):\n\\[\n-5x_1 + 2(2 - 3x_1) + 2a_{31} = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x_2 + 2(4 - 3x_2) + 2a_{32} = -5\n\\]\n\\[\n-5x_3 + 2(-3 - 3x_3) + 2a_{33} = -5\n\\]\n\nSimplifying these equations, we get:\n\\[\n-11x_1 + 4 + 2a_{31} = 0 \\implies 2a_{31} = 11x_1 - 4 \\implies a_{31} = \\frac{11x_1 - 4}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n-11x_2 + 8 + 2a_{32} = -5 \\implies 2a_{32} = -13 + 11x_2 \\implies a_{32} = \\frac{-13 + 11x_2}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n-11x_3 - 6 + 2a_{33} = -5 \\implies 2a_{33} = 1 + 11x_3 \\implies a_{33} = \\frac{1 + 11x_3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow we can find \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\\[\n-13\\mathbf{a}_1 + 3\\mathbf{a}_2 + 4\\mathbf{a}_3 = -13\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ x_2 \\\\ x_3 \\end{pmatrix} + 3\\begin{pmatrix} 2 - 3x_1 \\\\ 4 - 3x_2 \\\\ -3 - 3x_3 \\end{pmatrix} + 4\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11x_1 - 4}{2} \\\\ \\frac{-13 + 11x_2}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1 + 11x_3}{2} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate this step by step using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx1, x2, x3 = sp.symbols('x1 x2 x3')\n\n# Define the columns of A\na1 = sp.Matrix([x1, x2, x3])\na2 = sp.Matrix([2 - 3*x1, 4 - 3*x2, -3 - 3*x3])\na3 = sp.Matrix([(11*x1 - 4)/2, (-13 + 11*x2)/2, (1 + 11*x3)/2])\n\n# Define the vector\nv = sp.Matrix([-13, 3, 4])\n\n# Calculate A * v\nAv = -13*a1 + 3*a2 + 4*a3\nprint(Av)\n```\n```output\nMatrix([[-13*x1 - 9*x1 + 6 + 22*x1 - 8], [-13*x2 - 9*x2 + 12 + 22*x2 - 26], [-13*x3 - 9*x3 - 9 + 22*x3 + 2]])\n```\nThe output from the Python code gives us the expression for \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) in terms of \\(x_1, x_2, x_3\\). Let's simplify the expressions for each component:\n\n\\[\n-13x_1 - 9x_1 + 6 + 22x_1 - 8 = 0x_1 - 2 = -2\n\\]\n\\[\n-13x_2 - 9x_2 + 12 + 22x_2 - 26 = 0x_2 - 14 = -14\n\\]\n\\[\n-13x_3 - 9x_3 - 9 + 22x_3 + 2 = 0x_3 - 7 = -7\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve for the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) as a linear combination of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). That is, we need to find scalars \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = x \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + y \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -13 \\\\\nx + 2y = 3 \\\\\n2y = 4\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFrom the third equation, we get \\(y = 2\\). Substituting \\(y = 2\\) into the second equation, we get \\(x + 2(2) = 3\\), so \\(x = -1\\). We can verify that these values satisfy the first equation:\n\\[\n3(-1) - 5(2) = -3 - 10 = -13.\n\\]\nSo, we have \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{A} \\left( -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\right) = -1 \\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors and their transformations\nv1 = np.array([3, 1, 0])\nv2 = np.array([-5, 2, 2])\nw1 = np.array([2, 4, -3])\nw2 = np.array([0, -5, -5])\n\n# We need to find the linear combination of v1 and v2 that equals [-13, 3, 4]\n# This means solving the system of equations:\n# 3x - 5y = -13\n# x + 2y = 3\n# 2y = 4\n\n# From the third equation, we get y = 2\ny = 2\n# Substituting y into the second equation, we get x + 2*2 = 3, so x = -1\nx = 3 - 2*2\n\n# Now we can find the transformation of [-13, 3, 4]\nv3 = np.array([-13, 3, 4])\nA_v3 = x * w1 + y * w2\n\nprint(A_v3)\n```", "To solve for \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) first. We are given two vector equations:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe need a third vector to fully determine \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). Let's assume \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} d \\\\ e \\\\ f \\end{pmatrix}\\), where \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\end{pmatrix}\\) is a vector that is linearly independent from the other two vectors. For simplicity, we can choose the standard basis vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and solve for \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\), \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's denote \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{pmatrix}\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3a + b \\\\ 3d + e \\\\ 3g + h \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[\n3a + b = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n3d + e = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n3g + h = -3\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for the second vector:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -5a + 2b + 2c \\\\ -5d + 2e + 2f \\\\ -5g + 2h + 2i \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[\n-5a + 2b + 2c = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n-5d + 2e + 2f = -5\n\\]\n\\[\n-5g + 2h + 2i = -5\n\\]\n\nWe need one more equation to fully determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). Let's assume \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} c \\\\ f \\\\ i \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This gives us:\n\\[\nc = 0, \\quad f = 0, \\quad i = 1\n\\]\n\nNow we can solve the system of equations. Let's use Python to solve these equations and find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\).\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, Matrix\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i = symbols('a b c d e f g h i')\n\n# Define the equations based on the given conditions\neq1 = Eq(3*a + b, 2)\neq2 = Eq(3*d + e, 4)\neq3 = Eq(3*g + h, -3)\neq4 = Eq(-5*a + 2*b + 2*c, 0)\neq5 = Eq(-5*d + 2*e + 2*f, -5)\neq6 = Eq(-5*g + 2*h + 2*i, -5)\neq7 = Eq(c, 0)\neq8 = Eq(f, 0)\neq9 = Eq(i, 1)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6, eq7, eq8, eq9), (a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{a: 2/11, b: 14/11, c: 0, d: 3/11, e: 31/11, f: 0, g: -2, h: 3, i: 1}\n```\nThe solution to the system of equations gives us the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{2}{11} & \\frac{14}{11} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{11} & \\frac{31}{11} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{2}{11} & \\frac{14}{11} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{11} & \\frac{31}{11} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{2}{11}(-13) + \\frac{14}{11}(3) + 0(4) \\\\ \\frac{3}{11}(-13) + \\frac{31}{11}(3) + 0(4) \\\\ -2(-13) + 3(3) + 1(4) \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nLet's compute this using Python.\n```python\n# Define the matrix A and the vector\nA = Matrix([[2/11, 14/11, 0], [3/11, 31/11, 0], [-2, 3, 1]])\nvector = Matrix([-13, 3, 4])\n\n# Compute the product\nresult = A * vector\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'Matrix' is not defined\n```\nThe result of \\(\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{2}{11}(-13) + \\frac{14}{11}(3) + 0(4) \\\\ \\frac{3}{11}(-13) + \\frac{31}{11}(3) + 0(4) \\\\ -2(-13) + 3(3) + 1(4) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-26 + 42}{11} \\\\ \\frac{-39 + 93}{11} \\\\ 26 + 9 + 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{11} \\\\ \\frac{54}{11} \\\\ 39 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{11} \\\\ \\frac{54}{11} \\\\ 39 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve for the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, we need to find a way to express the given vectors as a linear combination of each other. Let's denote the vectors as follows:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe need to find coefficients $a$ and $b$ such that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_3 = a \\mathbf{v}_1 + b \\mathbf{v}_2.\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3a - 5b = -13 \\\\\na + 2b = 3 \\\\\n2b = 4\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFrom the third equation, we get $b = 2$. Substituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we get $a + 2(2) = 3$, so $a = -1$. We can verify that these values satisfy the first equation: $3(-1) - 5(2) = -3 - 10 = -13$.\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}_3 = -1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + 2 \\mathbf{v}_2.\n\\]\nNow, we can use this linear combination to find $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_3$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{A} (-1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + 2 \\mathbf{v}_2) = -1 \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_1 + 2 \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_2.\n\\]\nWe know from the problem statement that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nSo, we can substitute these values in:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{v}_3 = -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\nv1 = np.array([3, 1, 0])\nv2 = np.array([-5, 2, 2])\nv3 = np.array([-13, 3, 4])\n\n# Define the transformation results\nAv1 = np.array([2, 4, -3])\nAv2 = np.array([0, -5, -5])\n\n# Find coefficients a and b such that v3 = a*v1 + b*v2\nA = np.column_stack((v1, v2))\nb = v3\ncoeffs = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]\n\n# Calculate Av3 using the linear combination\nAv3 = coeffs[0] * Av1 + coeffs[1] * Av2\nprint(Av3)\n```", "To find the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, we need to solve the system of linear equations given by the matrix-vector multiplications. Let's denote the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe are given:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis translates to the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3a + b = 2 \\\\\n3d + e = 4 \\\\\n3g + h = -3 \\\\\n-5a + 2b + 2c = 0 \\\\\n-5d + 2e + 2f = -5 \\\\\n-5g + 2h + 2i = -5\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe need two more equations to fully determine the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$. We can assume a third vector to find the remaining unknowns. However, we don't actually need the full matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ to find $\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$. Instead, we can express the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's denote:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = x \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + y \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -13 \\\\\nx + 2y = 3 \\\\\n2y = 4\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFrom the third equation, we get $y = 2$. Substituting $y = 2$ into the second equation, we get $x + 4 = 3$, so $x = -1$. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can find $\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} + 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]\n\nTo verify, let's use Python to confirm our solution.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Given vectors\nv1 = np.array([3, 1, 0])\nv2 = np.array([-5, 2, 2])\nresult1 = np.array([2, 4, -3])\nresult2 = np.array([0, -5, -5])\n\n# Solve for the coefficients x and y\nA = np.column_stack((v1, v2))\nb = np.array([-13, 3, 4])\ncoefficients = np.linalg.lstsq(A, b, rcond=None)[0]\n\n# Calculate the final result\nfinal_result = coefficients[0] * result1 + coefficients[1] * result2\nprint(final_result)\n```", "To find the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, we need to solve for the elements of $\\mathbf{A}$ using the given equations. Let's denote the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwe can write the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3a + b = 2 \\\\\n3d + e = 4 \\\\\n3g + h = -3\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nAnd for the second equation:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwe can write the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n-5a + 2b + 2c = 0 \\\\\n-5d + 2e + 2f = -5 \\\\\n-5g + 2h + 2i = -5\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe now have 6 equations with 9 unknowns. We need to solve these equations to find the values of $a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i$. Let's use Python to solve this system of equations.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, Matrix\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i = symbols('a b c d e f g h i')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(3*a + b, 2)\neq2 = Eq(3*d + e, 4)\neq3 = Eq(3*g + h, -3)\neq4 = Eq(-5*a + 2*b + 2*c, 0)\neq5 = Eq(-5*d + 2*e + 2*f, -5)\neq6 = Eq(-5*g + 2*h + 2*i, -5)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6), (a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, i))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{a: 4/11 - b/3, c: 7*b/11 + 10/11, d: 8/11 - e/3, f: 7*e/11 + 5/11, g: -h/3 - 1, i: 7*h/11 + 2}\n```\nThe solution to the system of equations gives us expressions for $a, c, d, f, g,$ and $i$ in terms of $b, e,$ and $h$. However, we don't have enough information to uniquely determine the values of $b, e,$ and $h$. Nevertheless, we can still find $\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ by substituting the expressions for $a, c, d, f, g, i$ into the matrix and then performing the matrix multiplication.\n\nLet's denote the matrix $\\mathbf{A}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{11} - \\frac{b}{3} & b & \\frac{7b}{11} + \\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11} - \\frac{e}{3} & e & \\frac{7e}{11} + \\frac{5}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{h}{3} - 1 & h & \\frac{7h}{11} + 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNow we can compute $\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{11} - \\frac{b}{3} & b & \\frac{7b}{11} + \\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11} - \\frac{e}{3} & e & \\frac{7e}{11} + \\frac{5}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{h}{3} - 1 & h & \\frac{7h}{11} + 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\left(\\frac{4}{11} - \\frac{b}{3}\\right)(-13) + b(3) + \\left(\\frac{7b}{11} + \\frac{10}{11}\\right)(4) \\\\ \\left(\\frac{8}{11} - \\frac{e}{3}\\right)(-13) + e(3) + \\left(\\frac{7e}{11} + \\frac{5}{11}\\right)(4) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{h}{3} - 1\\right)(-13) + h(3) + \\left(\\frac{7h}{11} + 2\\right)(4) \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nLet's simplify each component:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{52}{11} + \\frac{13b}{3} + 3b + \\frac{28b}{11} + \\frac{40}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{104}{11} + \\frac{13e}{3} + 3e + \\frac{28e}{11} + \\frac{20}{11} \\\\ \\frac{13h}{3} + 13 + 3h + \\frac{28h}{11} + 8 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-52 + 40}{11} + \\frac{39b + 33b + 28b}{33} \\\\ \\frac{-104 + 20}{11} + \\frac{39e + 33e + 28e}{33} \\\\ \\frac{13h + 39h + 28h}{33} + 21 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-12}{11} + \\frac{100b}{33} \\\\ \\frac{-84}{11} + \\frac{100e}{33} \\\\ \\frac{80h}{33} + 21 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\