firm this by writing a Python code to verify the result.\n```python\n# Define the parameterized line equations\ndef x(t):\n    return 8 + 2 * t\n\ndef y(t):\n    return -1 + 3 * t\n\n# Choose a range of t values to verify the line equation\nt_values = range(-10, 11)  # from -10 to 10\n\n# Check if the points (x(t), y(t)) satisfy the line equation y = (3/2)x - 13\nresults = []\nfor t in t_values:\n    x_val = x(t)\n    y_val = y(t)\n    expected_y = (3/2) * x_val - 13\n    results.append((x_val, y_val, expected_y, y_val == expected_y))\n\n# Display the results\nprint(results)\n```\n```output\n[(-12, -31, -31.0, True), (-10, -28, -28.0, True), (-8, -25, -25.0, True), (-6, -22, -22.0, True), (-4, -19, -19.0, True), (-2, ...1, 10.0, True), (16, 22, 22.0, True), (18, 25, 25.0, True), (20, 28, 28.0, True), (22, 31, 31.0, True), (24, 34, 34.0, True)]\n```\nThe results show that for each value of $t$, the points $(x(t), y(t))$ satisfy the line equation $y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 13$. This confirms that the equation of the line is indeed $y = \\frac{3}{2}x - 13$, and the ordered pair $(m, b)$ is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -13\\right)}\n\\]", "The given parameterization of the line is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis means that the coordinates \\((x, y)\\) of any point on the line can be written as:\n\\[\nx = 8 + 2t \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -1 + 3t.\n\\]\nTo express the line in the form \\(y = mx + b\\), we need to eliminate the parameter \\(t\\). We can solve the first equation for \\(t\\):\n\\[\nt = \\frac{x - 8}{2}.\n\\]\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(t\\) into the second equation:\n\\[\ny = -1 + 3\\left(\\frac{x - 8}{2}\\right).\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\ny = -1 + \\frac{3x - 24}{2} = -1 + \\frac{3x}{2} - 12 = \\frac{3x}{2} - 13.\n\\]\nSo, the equation of the line i