ad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange multipliers.]} \\quad \\text{[Hint: Use the method of Lagrange', "Evaluate $1^2-2^2+3^2-4^2+\\cdots+99^2-100^2+101$ without a calculator. \\boxed{}. Shay\nTo solve the problem, we need to evaluate the expression \\(1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + \\cdots + 99^2 - 100^2 + 101\\).\n\nFirst, let's observe the pattern in the series. The series can be written as:\n\\[1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + \\cdots + 99^2 - 100^2 + 101.\\]\n\nWe can group the terms in pairs (except for the last term):\n\\[(1^2 - 2^2) + (3^2 - 4^2) + \\cdots + (99^2 - 100^2) + 101.\\]\n\nEach pair \\(n^2 - (n+1)^2\\) can be simplified as:\n\\[n^2 - (n+1)^2 = n^2 - (n^2 + 2n + 1) = -2n - 1.\\]\n\nSo the series becomes:\n\\[-(2 \\cdot 1 + 1) - (2 \\cdot 3 + 1) - \\cdots - (2 \\cdot 99 + 1) + 101.\\]\n\nThis is equivalent to:\n\\[-(2 \\cdot 1 + 1) - (2 \\cdot 3 + 1) - \\cdots - (2 \\cdot 99 + 1) + 101 = -\\sum_{k=1}^{50} (2(2k-1) + 1) + 101.\\]\n\nSimplifying the sum inside the series:\n\\[-\\sum_{k=1}^{50} (4k - 1) + 101 = -\\left(4\\sum_{k=1}^{50} k - \\sum_{k=1}^{50} 1\\right) + 101 = -\\left(4 \\cdot \\frac{50 \\cdot 51}{2} - 50\\right) + 101.\\]\n\nNow we can compute the value:\n\\[-\\left(4 \\cdot \\frac{50 \\cdot 51}{2} - 50\\right) + 101 = -\\left(4 \\cdot 1275 - 50\\right) + 101 = -(5100 - 50) + 101 = -5050 + 101 = -4949.\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Calculate the series 1^2 - 2^2 + 3^2 - 4^2 + ... + 99^2 - 100^2 + 101\nresult = 0\nfor i in range(1, 101):\n    if i % 2 == 1:  # odd index, add the square\n        result += i**2\n    else:  # even index, subtract the square\n        result -= i**2\nresult += 101  # add the last term which is not part of the pairs\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n-4949\n```\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{-4949}\\).", 'Compute: $1.\\overline{2}-0.\\overline{3}+0.\\overline{5}$. Express your answer as a common fraction. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}.