+ 128^\\circ = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation:\n\n\\[3x^\\circ + 128^\\circ = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nSubtract 128 from both sides:\n\n\\[3x^\\circ = 52^\\circ\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[x^\\circ = \\frac{52^\\circ}{3}\\]\n\nNow we can find the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\). Since \\(x^\\circ\\) and \\(2x^\\circ\\) are given angles and they form a linear pair with \\(\\angle PQR\\), we can use the value of \\(x\\) to find the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\).\n\nHowever, since \\(\\angle PQR\\) is already given as \\(128^\\circ\\), we can directly state the answer.\n\nLet's use Python to verify the calculation.\n```python\n# Calculation to verify the value of x\r\nx = 52 / 3\r\n\r\n# Given angle PQR\r\nangle_PQR = 128\r\n\r\n# Print the value of x and the angle PQR\r\nprint((x, angle_PQR))\n```\n```output\n(17.333333333333332, 128)\n```\nThe value of \\(x\\) is approximately \\(17.33^\\circ\\), and the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\) is \\(128^\\circ\\).\n\nTherefore, the measure of \\(\\angle PQR\\) is \\(\\boxed{128}\\) degrees.", "Given the diagram, we have the following information:\n\n1. \\( PT \\) is parallel to \\( QR \\).\n2. \\( \\angle P \\) is given as \\( x^\\circ \\).\n3. \\( \\angle T \\) is given as \\( 2x^\\circ \\).\n4. \\( \\angle Q \\) is given as \\( 128^\\circ \\).\n\nSince \\( PT \\) is parallel to \\( QR \\), the corresponding angles and alternate interior angles will be equal. We can use the properties of parallel lines and the angles they form to solve for \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, let's identify the relevant angles:\n- \\( \\angle P \\) and \\( \\angle T \\) are on a straight line with \\( \\angle Q \\).\n- The sum of angles around a point is \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n\nWe can set up the equation based on the given angles and the properties of parallel lines:\n\n\\[\nx + 2x + 128 = 180\n\\]\n\nSolving this equation will give us the value of \\( x \\). Let's solve it step-by-step using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n