n \\(\\frac{1331}{9999}\\) to its lowest terms. To do this, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1331 and 9999. We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD.\n\nThe Euclidean algorithm works as follows:\n1. Divide 9999 by 1331 and find the remainder.\n2. Replace 9999 with 1331 and 1331 with the remainder from the previous step.\n3. Repeat until the remainder is 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\nLet's perform these steps:\n\n\\[9999 \\div 1331 = 7 \\text{ with remainder } 882\\]\n\\[1331 \\div 882 = 1 \\text{ with remainder } 449\\]\n\\[882 \\div 449 = 1 \\text{ with remainder } 433\\]\n\\[449 \\div 433 = 1 \\text{ with remainder } 16\\]\n\\[433 \\div 16 = 27 \\text{ with remainder } 1\\]\n\\[16 \\div 1 = 16 \\text{ with remainder } 0\\]\n\nSo, the GCD of 1331 and 9999 is 11. Dividing both the numerator and the denominator by 11, we get:\n\n\\[\\frac{1331 \\div 11}{9999 \\div 11} = \\frac{121}{909}\\]\n\nSince 121 and 909 have no common factors other than 1, the fraction \\(\\frac{121}{909}\\) is in its simplest form. Therefore, \\(a = 121\\) and \\(b = 909\\), and \\(a + b = 121 + 909 = 1030\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{1030}\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Values\nnumerator = 1331\ndenominator = 9999\n\n# Calculate GCD\ngcd = math.gcd(numerator, denominator)\n\n# Simplify fraction\na = numerator // gcd\nb = denominator // gcd\n\n# Calculate a + b\nresult = a + b\nprint(result)\n```", "To convert the repeating decimal \\(0.\\overline{1331}\\) to a fraction, we can use algebraic manipulation. Let's denote \\(x = 0.\\overline{1331}\\). Then, we can write:\n\n\\[10000x = 1331.\\overline{1331}\\]\n\nNow, subtract the original \\(x\\) from this equation:\n\n\\[10000x - x = 1331.\\overline{1331} - 0.\\overline{1331}\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[9999x = 1331\\]\n\nSo, solving for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x = \\frac{1331}{9999}\\]\n\nNext, we need to simplify the fraction \\