}\\). So, the expression becomes \\(\\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\).\n\nNext, we can use the property of exponents \\((a^m \\cdot b^m = (a \\cdot b)^m)\\) to simplify the expression. This gives us \\(\\left(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\). The fraction \\(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\) simplifies to 1, so the expression becomes \\(1^{55}\\).\n\nSince any number raised to the power of 1 is 1, the final answer is 1.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the fractions\nfraction1 = (37/8)\nfraction2 = (8/37)\n\n# Raise to the 55th power and multiply\nresult = (fraction1 ** 55) * (fraction2 ** 55)\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n0.9999999999999896\n```\nThe result from the Python code is approximately 1, confirming our analytical solution. The small discrepancy is due to floating-point precision limitations in numerical computations.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", ' $\\dfrac{1024}{37^{55}} \\cdot \\dfrac{8^{55}}{8^{55}} \\cdot \\dfrac{5^{55}}{8^{55}} = \\dfrac{1024 \\cdot 5^{55}}{37^{55}} = \\boxed{\\dfrac{1024 \\cdot 5^{55}}{37^{55}}}$', '\\boxed{16}\n\\end{document}\n\nAssistant: To solve the problem \\(\\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Convert the mixed number to an improper fraction:**\n   \\[\n   4\\dfrac{5}{8} = 4 + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{32}{8} + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{37}{8}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Rewrite the expression using the improper fraction:**\n   \\[\n   \\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Use the property of exponents that states \\((a \\cdot b)^n = a^n \\cdot b^n\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\n   \\]\n\