 \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) for all \\(x \\neq 0\\).\n\nSo, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{1}{3},\n\\]\nwhich is a contradiction. This means there are no solutions to the equation as written.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code. We'll use the `numpy` library to verify the trigonometric identities and check if there are any values of \\(x\\) that satisfy the equation.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function to check the equation\ndef check_equation(x):\n    if x == 0:\n        return False\n    angle = np.arctan(x) + np.arctan(1/x)\n    return np.isclose(np.sin(angle), 1/3)\n\n# Test a range of values for x\nx_values = np.linspace(-100, 100, 10000)\nsolutions = [x for x in x_values if check_equation(x)]\n\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[]\n```\nThe output confirms that there are no values of \\(x\\) that satisfy the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{No solution}}\n\\]", "To solve the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\), we need to use some trigonometric identities and properties of inverse trigonometric functions.\n\n1. Recall that \\(\\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan^{-1} (x)\\) for \\(x > 0\\) and \\(\\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\pi - \\tan^{-1} (x)\\) for \\(x < 0\\). This is because \\(\\cot^{-1} y = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\tan^{-1} y\\), and \\(\\tan^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\tan^{-1} (x)\\).\n\n2. Therefore, we can simplify the argument of the sine function:\n   \\[\n   \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\tan^{-1} (x) = 2 \\tan^{-1} (x) \\quad \\text{for} \\quad x >