ltiple sets of positive integer roots that satisfy the equations above for the same value of $a$. Jon is able to determine the value of $a$ but not $c$, which implies that for the given value of $a$, there are multiple possible values of $c$. This means that there are multiple sets of roots that satisfy the equations for the same $a$.\n\nLet's find the possible values of $a$ and $c$ by iterating through possible values of $a$ and checking the conditions. We will use Python to automate this process.\n\nWe will start by iterating through possible values of $a$ and checking if there are multiple sets of roots that satisfy the conditions. Then, we will find the corresponding values of $c$ and sum the two possible values.\n\nLet's write the code to find the solution.\n```python\nfrom itertools import combinations_with_replacement\n\ndef find_possible_c_values():\n    possible_c_values = []\n    # We will check for values of a from 1 to a reasonable upper limit, say 100\n    for a in range(1, 101):\n        # Generate all possible combinations of roots that sum to a\n        roots = []\n        for r1 in range(1, a):\n            for r2 in range(1, a - r1 + 1):\n                r3 = a - r1 - r2\n                if r3 > 0:\n                    roots.append((r1, r2, r3))\n        \n        # Check if there are multiple sets of roots that satisfy the conditions\n        valid_c_values = set()\n        for r1, r2, r3 in roots:\n            if (r1 * r2 + r2 * r3 + r3 * r1) * 2 == a**2 - 81:\n                c = 2 * r1 * r2 * r3\n                valid_c_values.add(c)\n        \n        # If there are multiple valid c values, add them to the list\n        if len(valid_c_values) > 1:\n            possible_c_values.extend(valid_c_values)\n    \n    # Return the sum of the two possible values of c\n    return sum(set(possible_c_values))\n\n# Find the sum of the two possible values of c\nsum_of_possible_c_values = find_possible_c_values()\nprint(sum_of_possible_c_values)\n```\n```ou