en the lines:**\n   - The distance \\(D\\) between two parallel lines can be found using the formula:\n     \\[\n     D = \\frac{|\\mathbf{n} \\cdot (\\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1})|}{|\\mathbf{n}|}\n     \\]\n     where \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) is a vector perpendicular to the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\).\n\n   - Since \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\) are parallel, we can use \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1}\\) (or \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\)).\n\n4. **Calculate the distance:**\n   - First, find the vector \\(\\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\n     \\]\n   - Then, compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{n} \\cdot (\\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1})\\):\n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot (\\mathbf{r_2} - \\mathbf{r_1}) = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = 2(-8) + (-9)(7) + (-2)(5) = -16 - 63 - 10 = -89\n     \\]\n   - Compute the magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\):\n     \\[\n     |\\mathbf{d_1}| = \\sqrt{2^2 + (-9)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 81 + 4} = \\sqrt{89}\n     \\]\n   - Finally, the distance \\(D\\) is:\n     \\[\n     D = \\frac{|-89|}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\frac{89}{\\sqrt{89}} = \\sqrt{89}\n     \\]\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify the result:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the direction vector and points on each line\nd1 = sp.Matrix([2, -9, -2])\nr1 = sp.Matrix([3, -10, 1])\nr2 = sp.Matrix([-5, -3, 6])\n\n# Calculate the vector r2 - r1\nr_diff = r2 - r1\n\n# Calculate the dot product d1 . (r2 - r1)\ndot_product = d1.dot(r_diff)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of d1\nmagnitude_d1 = sp.sqrt(d1.dot(d1))\n\n# Calculate the distance D\ndistance = sp.Abs(dot_product) / magnitude_d1\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\nsqrt(89)\n```\nThe distance between the two parallel lines is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{89}}\\).", "To find the distance between two parallel lines, we can use the following formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Distance} = \\frac{|(\\mathbf{r_2}(0) - \\mathbf{r_1}(0)) \\cdot \\mathbf{n}|}{|\\mathbf{n}|} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\mathbf{r_1}(0)\\) and \\(\\mathbf{r_2}(0)\\) are the points on the lines when the parameter is 0,\n- \\(\\mathbf{n}\\) is the cross product of the direction vectors of the lines.\n\nLet's break this down step-by-step:\n\n1. Identify the points on the lines when the parameter is 0:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r_1}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{r_2}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n2. Find the direction vectors of the lines:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{d_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{d_2} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. Compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -9 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -18 & -4 \\end{vmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n4. Compute the vector \\(\\mathbf{r_2}(0) - \\mathbf{r_1}(0)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r_2}(0) - \\mathbf{r_1}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 - 3 \\\\ -3 - (-10) \\\\ 6 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n5. Compute the dot product \\((\\mathbf{r_2}(0) - \\mathbf{r_1}(0)) \\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\).\n\n6. Compute the magnitude of \\(\\mathbf{n}\\).\n\n7. Finally, use the formula to find the distance.\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the points on the lines when the parameter is 0\nr1_0 = sp.Matrix([3, -10, 1])\nr2_0 = sp.Matrix([-5, -3, 6])\n\n# Define the direction vectors of the lines\nd1 = sp.Matrix([2, -9, -2])\nd2 = sp.Matrix([4, -18, -4])\n\n# Compute the cross product n = d1 x d2\nn = d1.cross(d2)\n\n# Compute the vector r2(0) - r1(0)\nr_diff = r2_0 - r1_0\n\n# Compute the dot product (r2(0) - r1(0)) . n\ndot_product = r_diff.dot(n)\n\n# Compute the magnitude of n\nn_magnitude = sp.sqrt(n.dot(n))\n\n# Compute the distance\ndistance = sp.Abs(dot_product) / n_magnitude\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\nnan\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the calculation, likely due to a division by zero or an undefined operation. Let's carefully re-evaluate the steps and ensure that all calculations are correct.\n\nFirst, let's recompute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -9 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -18 & -4 \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe cross product is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{i}((-9)(-4) - (-2)(-18)) - \\mathbf{j}((2)(-4) - (-2)(4)) + \\mathbf{k}((2)(-18) - (-9)(4))\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each component:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{i}(36 - 36) - \\mathbf{j}(-8 + 8) + \\mathbf{k}(-36 + 36)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{i}(0) - \\mathbf{j}(0) + \\mathbf{k}(0)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{n} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe cross product is the zero vector, which means the lines are actually the same line or they are parallel but coincident. In this case, the distance between the lines is zero. However, since the lines are distinct and parallel, the distance should be a non-zero value.\n\nLet's recompute the steps to ensure there are no mistakes:\n\n1. Points on the lines when the parameter is 0:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r_1}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{r_2}(0) = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n2. Direction vectors:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{d_1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{d_2} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. Cross product:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{n} = \\mathbf{d_1} \\times \\mathbf{d_2} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \