p\n\n# Define the points on the lines\nP1 = sp.Matrix([3, -10, 1])\nP2 = sp.Matrix([-5, -3, 6])\n\n# Define the direction vectors of the lines\nd1 = sp.Matrix([2, -9, -2])\nd2 = sp.Matrix([4, -18, -4])\n\n# Compute the vector between the points on the lines\nP2_minus_P1 = P2 - P1\n\n# Compute the cross product of the direction vectors\ncross_product = d1.cross(d2)\n\n# Compute the magnitude of the cross product\nmagnitude_cross_product = cross_product.norm()\n\n# Compute the dot product of the vector between the points and the cross product\ndot_product = P2_minus_P1.dot(cross_product)\n\n# Compute the distance\ndistance = sp.Abs(dot_product) / magnitude_cross_product\n\nprint(distance)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nThe output indicates that the distance between the two lines is \\(0\\). This suggests that the lines are actually coincident, meaning they are the same line. \n\nTo confirm this, let's re-examine the direction vectors and the points:\n\n1. **Points on the lines:**\n   - Point on Line 1: \\(\\mathbf{P}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n   - Point on Line 2: \\(\\mathbf{P}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\n2. **Direction vectors:**\n   - Direction vector of Line 1: \\(\\mathbf{d}_1 = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n   - Direction vector of Line 2: \\(\\mathbf{d}_2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n\nNotice that \\(\\mathbf{d}_2 = 2 \\mathbf{d}_1\\), which means the direction vectors are scalar multiples of each other. This confirms that the lines are parallel.\n\nNow, let's check if the lines are coincident by verifying if there exists a scalar \\(k\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P}_2 - \\mathbf{P}_1 = k \\mathbf{d}_1\n\\]\n\nLet's solve for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -5 - 3 \\\\ -3 + 10 \\\\ 6 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = k \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = k \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -9 \\\\