(0 < r < 3\\), let's compare the values of \\(\\ln(1 + k)\\) for each \\(k\\):\n\n- \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\)\n- \\(\\ln(1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\)\n- \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\)\n- \\(\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\)\n- \\(\\ln(1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\)\n\nSince the natural logarithm function \\(\\ln\\) is an increasing function, the larger the value of \\(1 + k\\), the larger the value of \\(\\ln(1 + k)\\). Therefore, we need to compare \\(1 + k\\) for each \\(k\\):\n\n- \\(1 + r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n- \\(1 + 2r\\)\n- \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\)\n- \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\)\n\nSince \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can see that:\n\n- \\(1 + 2r > 1 + r > 1 + \\sqrt{r} > 1 + \\frac{1}{r} > 1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\)\n\nTherefore, \\(\\ln(1 + 2r)\\) is the largest among all the values, which means \\(x\\) is the smallest for equation (C). The next largest is \\(\\ln(1 + r)\\), so \\(x\\) is the next smallest for equation (A). The next largest is \\(\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})\\), so \\(x\\) is the next smallest for equation (D). The next largest is \\(\\ln(1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\), so \\(x\\) is the next smallest for equation (E). The smallest is \\(\\ln(1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\), so \\(x\\) is the largest for equation (B).\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by calculating the values using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the value of r within the range 0 < r < 3\r\nr = 1.5  # Example value of r\r\n\r\n# Calculate the values of k for each equation\r\nk_values = {\r\n    'A': r,\r\n    'B': r / 10,\r\n    'C': 2 * r,\r\n    'D': np.sqrt(r),\r\n    'E': 1 / r\r\n}\r\n\r\n# Calculate the natural logarithm of (1 + k) for each k\r\nln_values = {key: np.log(1 + value) for key, value in k_values.items()}\r\n\r\n# The value of x is inversely proportional to ln(1 + k), so we need to find the minimum ln(1 + k)\r\nmin_ln_value = min(ln_values.values())\r\nmax_x_equation = [key for key, value in ln_values.items() if value == min_ln_value][0]\r\n\r\nprint(max_x_equation)\n```", "To determine which equation has the largest solution \\( x \\) for \\( 0 < r < 3 \\), we can start by solving each equation for \\( x \\) and then comparing the solutions. The general form of each equation is \\( 3(1 + r')^x = 7 \\), where \\( r' \\) is a different expression of \\( r \\) for each option.\n\nRewriting each equation in terms of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n3(1 + r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right)}{\\log (1 + r)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3(1 + 2r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right)}{\\log (1 + 2r)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right)}{\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\log \\left( \\frac{7}{3} \\right) \\) is a constant, the value of \\( x \\) is inversely proportional to \\( \\log (1 + r') \\). Therefore, we need to compare \\( \\log (1 + r) \\), \\( \\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10}) \\), \\( \\log (1 + 2r) \\), \\( \\log (1 + \\sqrt{r}) \\), and \\( \\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r}) \\) for \\( 0 < r < 3 \\).\n\nSince the logarithm function is monotonically increasing, we can compare the arguments directly:\n\n1. \\( 1 + r \\)\n2. \\( 1 + \\frac{r}{10} \\)\n3. \\( 1 + 2r \\)\n4. \\( 1 + \\sqrt{r} \\)\n5. \\( 1 + \\frac{1}{r} \\)\n\nFor \\( 0 < r < 3 \\):\n\n- \\( 1 + r \\) is between 1 and 4.\n- \\( 1 + \\frac{r}{10} \\) is between 1 and 1.3.\n- \\( 1 + 2r \\) is between 1 and 7.\n- \\( 1 + \\sqrt{r} \\) is between 1 and approximately 2.8.\n- \\( 1 + \\frac{1}{r} \\) is between 1 and approximately 2 (since 