es of \\(x\\) for each of the given equations. Let\'s define the equations and solve for \\(x\\) using Python.\n\n1. For \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\), we have \\(r\' = r\\).\n2. For \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\), we have \\(r\' = \\frac{r}{10}\\).\n3. For \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\), we have \\(r\' = 2r\\).\n4. For \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\), we have \\(r\' = \\sqrt{r}\\).\n5. For \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7\\), we have \\(r\' = \\frac{1}{r}\\).\n\nWe will now write Python code to compute the values of \\(x\\) for each of these equations and determine which one has the largest solution.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the function to compute x\ndef compute_x(r_prime):\n    return math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + r_prime)\n\n# Define the values of r_prime for each equation\nr_prime_values = {\n    "A": 1 + r,\n    "B": 1 + r/10,\n    "C": 1 + 2*r,\n    "D": 1 + math.sqrt(r),\n    "E": 1 + 1/r\n}\n\n# Compute the values of x for each equation\nx_values = {key: compute_x(value) for key, value in r_prime_values.items()}\n\n# Find the equation with the largest solution x\nmax_x_equation = max(x_values, key=x_values.get)\nmax_x_value = x_values[max_x_equation]\n\nprint((max_x_equation, max_x_value))\n```\n```output\nNameError: name \'r\' is not defined\n```\nIt seems I didn\'t define the value of \\(r\\) in the code. Since \\(0 < r < 3\\), let\'s choose a specific value for \\(r\\) within this range, for example, \\(r = 1\\). We can then compute the values of \\(x\\) for each equation using this specific value of \\(r\\).\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the function to compute x\ndef compute_x(r_prime):\n    return math.log(7/3) / math.log(1 + r_prime)\n\n# Define the value of r\nr = 1\n\n# Define the values of r_prime for each equation\nr_prime_values = {\n    "A": 1 + r,\n    "B": 1 + r/10,\n    "C": 1 + 2*r,\n    "D": 1 + math.sqrt(r),\n    "E": 1 + 1/r\n}\n\n# Compute the values of x for each equation\nx_values = {key: compute_x(value) for key, value in r_prime_values.items()}\n\n# Find the equation with the largest solution x\nmax_x_equation = max(x_values, key=x_values.get)\nmax_x_value = x_values[max_x_equation]\n\nprint((max_x_equation, max_x_value))\n```\n```output\n(\'B\', 1.557046340166736)\n```\nThe equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) when \\(r = 1\\) is \\(\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7\\), with \\(x \\approx 1.557\\).\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).', "To solve this problem, we need to compare the values of \\(x\\) for each of the given equations when \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\), \\(3(1 + r/10)^x = 7\\), \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7\\), \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7\\), and \\(3(1 + 1/r)^x = 7\\). We can isolate \\(x\\) in each equation and compare the values.\n\nLet's rewrite each equation in terms of \\(x\\):\n\n1. \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + r)}\\)\n2. \\(3(1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{r}{10})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})}\\)\n3. \\(3(1 + 2r)^x = 7 \\implies (1 + 2r)^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + 2r)}\\)\n4. \\(3(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\sqrt{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})}\\)\n5. \\(3(1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = 7 \\implies (1 + \\frac{1}{r})^x = \\frac{7}{3} \\implies x = \\frac{\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})}\\)\n\nSince \\(\\log \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, we need to compare the denominators \\(\\log (1 + r)\\), \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{r}{10})\\), \\(\\log (1 + 2r)\\), \\(\\log (1 + \\sqrt{r})\\), and \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\). The larger the denominator, the smaller the value of \\(x\\).\n\nLet's analyze the denominators:\n\n- Since \\(0 < r < 3\\), \\(1 + r < 1 + 2r < 1 + \\sqrt{r} < 1 + \\frac{1}{r} < 1 + 3 = 4\\).\n- The function \\(\\log\\) is increasing, so \\(\\log (1 + r) < \\log (1 + 2r) < \\log (1 + \\sqrt{r}) < \\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r}) < \\log 4\\).\n\nThus, the smallest denominator is \\(\\log (1 + r)\\), and the largest denominator is \\(\\log (1 + \\frac{1}{r})\\). Therefore, the largest value of \\(x\\) is obtained when the denominator is the smallest, which is \\(\\log (1 + r)\\).\n\nSo, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is \\(3(1 + r)^x = 7\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).", 'The function $f$, defined on the set of ordered pairs of positive integers, satisfies the following properties: \\begin{align*}  f(x,x) &=x, \\\\  f(x,y) &=f(y,x), \\quad \\text{and} \\\\  (x + y) f(x,y) &= yf(x,x + y).  \\end{align*}Calculate $f(14,52)$.\n\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 22\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 26\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 34\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 36\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 40$  \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxe