 - a)(c - b)} \\). When we expand these terms, we get:\n\n\\[ \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} = \\frac{x^2 - (b+c)x + bc}{(a - b)(a - c)}, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} = \\frac{x^2 - (a+c)x + ac}{(b - a)(b - c)}, \\]\n\\[ \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)} = \\frac{x^2 - (a+b)x + ab}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nEach of these terms is a quadratic polynomial in \\(x\\), and when we sum them, the resulting polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is also a quadratic polynomial. Therefore, the degree of \\( p(x) \\) is 2.\n\nLet's confirm this by calculating \\( p(x) \\) in Python and examining its degree.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, expand, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\r\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the polynomial\r\np_x_simplified = simplify(p_x)\r\n\r\n# Get the degree of the polynomial\r\ndegree = p_x_simplified.as_poly().degree()\r\n\r\nprint((p_x_simplified, degree))\n```\n```output\n(1, 0)\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\( 1 \\), which is a constant polynomial. This means that the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is actually a constant function, and its degree is \\( 0 \\).\n\nTherefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "The given polynomial is:\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nTo determine the degree of the polynomial, let's analyze the expression step-by-step.\n\n1. Each term in the polynomial is a fraction where the numerator is a quadratic polynomial in \\(x\\), and the denominator is a constant. Therefore, each term is a quadratic polynomial in \\(x\\).\n\n2. The degree of a sum of polynomials is the maximum of the degrees of the individual polynomials, provided that the leading terms do not can