", "To simplify the given expression \\(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}+\\sqrt{45}}{\\sqrt{5}}}\\), we can break it down step by step.\n\n1. Simplify the individual components of the numerator:\n   - \\(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}}\\)\n   - \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9}\\)\n   - \\(\\sqrt{45}\\)\n\n2. Combine these simplified components and divide by \\(\\sqrt{5}\\).\n\n3. Finally, take the square root of the resulting fraction.\n\nLet's start by simplifying each component:\n\n1. \\(\\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{80}} = \\dfrac{5}{\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 5}} = \\dfrac{5}{4\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{5 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{4 \\cdot 5} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}\\)\n\n2. \\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{845}}{9} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{169 \\cdot 5}}{9} = \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9}\\)\n\n3. \\(\\sqrt{45} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\\)\n\nNow we can combine these components:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} + \\dfrac{13\\sqrt{5}}{9} + 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 4, 9, and 1 is 36:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{9\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{52\\sqrt{5}}{36} + \\dfrac{108\\sqrt{5}}{36} = \\dfrac{9\\sqrt{5} + 52\\sqrt{5} + 108\\sqrt{5}}{36} = \\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}\n\\]\n\nNow we divide this by \\(\\sqrt{5}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{169\\sqrt{5}}{36\\sqrt{5}} = \\dfrac{169}{36}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we take the square root of \\(\\dfrac{169}{36}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{169}{36}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{169}}{\\sqrt{36}} = \\dfrac{13}{6}\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified expression is \\(\\dfrac{13}{6}\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the expression\nnumerator = sp.Rational(5, sp.sqrt(80)) + sp.sqrt(845) / 9 + sp.sqrt(45)\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\nfraction = numerator / denominator\nsimplified_fraction = sp.simplify(fraction)\nfinal_result = sp.sqrt(simplified_fraction)\n\nprint(final_result)\n```\n```output\nTypeError: invalid input: 4*sqrt(5)\n```\nIt looks like there was an error in the code due to the way the expression was defined. Let's correct it by ensuring that the expression is properly simplified and then taking the square root. We'll use sympy's `simplify` and `sqrt` functions to handle the expression correctly.\n\nLet's rewrite the code to ensure it works properly.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the components of the numerator\nterm1 = sp.Rational(5, sp.sqrt(80))\nterm2 = sp.sqrt(845) / 9\nterm3 = sp.sqrt(45)\n\n# Combine the terms\nnumerator = term1 + term2 + term3\n\n# Define the denominator\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\n\n# Form the fraction and simplify\nfraction = numerator / denominator\nsimplified_fraction = sp.simplify(fraction)\n\n# Take the square root of the simplified fraction\nfinal_result = sp.sqrt(simplified_fraction)\n\nprint(final_result)\n```\n```output\nTypeError: invalid input: 4*sqrt(5)\n```\nIt seems there's still an issue with the way the expression is being handled. Let's break it down step-by-step and ensure that each component is properly simplified before combining them.\n\nWe'll manually simplify each component and then combine them, ensuring that we handle the square roots correctly. Let's rewrite the code to handle this properly.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the components of the numerator\nterm1 = 5 / sp.sqrt(80)\nterm2 = sp.sqrt(845) / 9\nterm3 = sp.sqrt(45)\n\n# Simplify each term\nterm1_simplified = sp.simplify(term1)\nterm2_simplified = sp.simplify(term2)\nterm3_simplified = sp.simplify(term3)\n\n# Combine the terms\nnumerator = term1_simplified + term2_simplified + term3_simplified\n\n# Define the denominator\ndenominator = sp.sqrt(5)\n\n# Form the fraction and simplify\nfraction = numerator / denominator\nsimplified_fraction = sp.simplify(fraction)\n\n# Take the square root of the simplified fraction\nfinal_result = sp.sqrt(simplified_fraction)\n\nprint(final_result)\n```\n```output\n13/6\n```\n\nReach max function call limit.", "To find the minimum value of 