en the number is divided by 10. Therefore, we can find the units digit of \\(19^6\\) by calculating \\(19^6 \\mod 10\\).\n\nSince the units digit of 19 is 9, we can simplify the problem to finding the units digit of \\(9^6\\). We can do this by calculating the units digit of the first few powers of 9 and looking for a pattern.\n\nLet's calculate the units digits of the first few powers of 9:\n- \\(9^1 = 9\\) (units digit is 9)\n- \\(9^2 = 81\\) (units digit is 1)\n- \\(9^3 = 729\\) (units digit is 9)\n- \\(9^4 = 6561\\) (units digit is 1)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 2 powers: 9, 1, 9, 1, ...\n\nSince the units digits repeat every 2 powers, we can determine the units digit of \\(9^6\\) by looking at the position of 6 in the repeating cycle. Since 6 is even, the units digit of \\(9^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(9^2\\), which is 1.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(19^6\\) is 1. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the units digit of 19^6\nunits_digit = (19 ** 6) % 10\nprint(units_digit)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe units digit of \\(19^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "Find the units digit of $24^4.$\n$\\textbf{(A) }0\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }2\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }4\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }6\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }8$\n\nTo find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. So, the problem reduces to finding the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nWe can determine the units digit of powers of 8 by observing the pattern in the units digits of the first few powers of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n8^1 & = 8 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^2 & = 64 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n8^3 & = 512 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 2) \\\\\n8^4 & = 4096 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 6) \\\\\n8^5 & = 32768 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the exponent 6 is divisible by 4 with a remainder of 2 (i.e., \\(6 \\div 4 = 1\\) with a remainder of 2), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\r\ndef units_digit(a, b):\r\n    # Get the units digit of the base\r\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\r\n    # Find the units digit of the power\r\n    units_digit_power = pow(units_digit_base, b, 10)\r\n    return units_digit_power\r\n\r\n# Calculate the units digit of 18^6\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\r\nprint(units_digit_18_6)\n```\n```output\n4\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "Find the units digit of $22^6.$\n/hide]\n\nTo find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. So, the problem reduces to finding the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nLet's calculate the units digits of the first few powers of 8:\n- \\(8^1 = 8\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\) (units digit is 4)\n- \\(8^3 = 512\\) (units digit is 2)\n- \\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6)\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^6 = 262144\\) (units digit is 4)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since \\(6 \\div 4 = 1\\) remainder 2, the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\r\ndef units_digit(a, b):\r\n    # Extract the units digit of the base\r\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\r\n    # Find the units digit of units_digit_base^b\r\n    units_digit_result = (units_digit_base ** b) % 10\r\n    return units_digit_result\r\n\r\n# Find the units digit of 18^6\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = un