d their relationships. Here\'s the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Determine the side length of the square**: Since the area of the square \\(ABCD\\) is 16 square inches, the side length of the square is \\(\\sqrt{16} = 4\\) inches.\n\n2. **Analyze the tangram pieces**: The tangram pieces consist of 7 pieces: 2 large isosceles right triangles, 1 medium isosceles right triangle, 2 small isosceles right triangles, and 1 square. The square "e" is one of these pieces.\n\n3. **Determine the area of the gray piece**: The gray piece is one of the small isosceles right triangles. To find its area, we need to understand the relative sizes of the pieces. The large isosceles right triangles each have legs of length 4 inches, so their area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times 4 = 8\\) square inches. The medium isosceles right triangle has legs of length 2 inches, so its area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 2 = 2\\) square inches. The small isosceles right triangles each have legs of length 1 inch, so their area is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\) square inches.\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the gray piece**: The gray piece is one of the small isosceles right triangles, so its area is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) square inches.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given data\nside_length_large_triangle = 4  # side length of the large isosceles right triangle\nside_length_medium_triangle = 2  # side length of the medium isosceles right triangle\nside_length_small_triangle = 1  # side length of the small isosceles right triangle\n\n# Calculate the area of the small isosceles right triangle\narea_small_triangle = 0.5 * side_length_small_triangle * side_length_small_triangle\n\nprint(area_small_triangle)\n```\n```output\n0.5\n```\nThe area of the gray piece, which is one of the small isosceles right triangles, is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) square inches.', 'To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometry of the tangram pieces and t