 are quite complex due to the nature of the roots. However, we can simplify these expressions by recognizing that the roots come in conjugate pairs. Let\'s denote the roots as follows:\n\\[\n\\alpha_1 = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}, \\quad \\alpha_2 = -\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_3 = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}, \\quad \\alpha_4 = -\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can see that $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_2$ are conjugates, and $\\alpha_3$ and $\\alpha_4$ are conjugates. This means that $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2$ and $\\alpha_3\\alpha_4$ are real numbers, and similarly for the other expressions. Let\'s simplify these expressions using the properties of conjugates.\n\nThe product of conjugates can be simplified as follows:\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 = \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}\\right) = \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4} - \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[4]{3}i}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{1 - 3\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, we can find that $\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = \\frac{1 - 3\\sqrt{3}}{4}$. Therefore, $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = \\frac{1 - 3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nNow let\'s simplify the other expressions. We can use the fact that the sum of the roots of the polynomial is $-2$, and the product of the roots taken two at a time is $0$. This means that the expressions $\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4$ and $\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3$ must be equal to the same value. Let\'