solve this system of congruences. The CRT states that if the moduli are pairwise coprime, then there is a unique solution modulo the product of the moduli. In this case, the moduli 7, 11, and 13 are pairwise coprime, so we can apply the CRT.\n\nFirst, let's find the solution to the system of congruences. We can express the solution in the form:\n\\[\nx = 10 + 143k\n\\]\nwhere 143 is the product of the moduli 11 and 13. We need to find the value of \\(k\\) such that \\(x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\\). Substituting \\(x = 10 + 143k\\) into the first congruence, we get:\n\\[\n10 + 143k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n10 + 2k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\implies 2k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\implies 2k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\implies k \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSo, \\(k = 7m + 1\\) for some integer \\(m\\). Substituting \\(k = 7m + 1\\) into the expression for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[\nx = 10 + 143(7m + 1) = 10 + 143 + 1001m = 153 + 1001m\n\\]\nTo find the largest integer less than 2010, we need to find the largest \\(m\\) such that \\(153 + 1001m < 2010\\). Solving for \\(m\\), we get:\n\\[\n1001m < 1857 \\implies m < \\frac{1857}{1001} \\approx 1.855\n\\]\nSo, the largest integer \\(m\\) is 1. Therefore, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is:\n\\[\nx = 153 + 1001 \\cdot 1 = 1154\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions\ndef find_largest_integer():\n    for m in range(1, 2010 // 1001 + 1):\n        x = 153 + 1001 * m\n        if x < 2010:\n            largest_x = x\n        else:\n            break\n    return largest_x\n\n# Find the largest integer\nlargest_integer = find_largest_integer()\nprint(largest_integer)\n```\n```output\n1154\n```\nThe largest integer less than 2010 that has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7, a remainder of 10 when divided by 11, and a remainder of 10 when divided by 13 is \\(\\boxed{1154}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nThis problem can be solved using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT). First, we need to find a solution to the system of congruences. Since the moduli 7, 11, and 13 are pairwise coprime, the CRT guarantees a unique solution modulo \\( 7 \\times 11 \\times 13 = 1001 \\).\n\nLet's find the solution step-by-step.\n\n1. We start by solving the last two congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\nSince both congruences have the same remainder, we can combine them into a single congruence:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nwhere 143 is the least common multiple of 11 and 13.\n\n2. Now we need to solve the system:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nUsing the method of successive substitutions, we can find a solution to this system.\n\nLet's write \\( x = 143k + 10 \\) and substitute it into the first congruence:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we can simplify this to:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6 (since \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\)). So we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\times 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nThis gives us the general solution to the system of congruences. The largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies these congruences is obtained by setting \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[ x = 1001 \\times 1 