} \\).\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to check the conditions\r\ndef check_conditions(x):\r\n    return (x % 7 == 5) and (x % 11 == 10) and (x % 13 == 10)\r\n\r\n# Iterate from 2009 down to 0 to find the largest x that satisfies the conditions\r\nfor x in range(2009, 0, -1):\r\n    if check_conditions(x):\r\n        largest_x = x\r\n        break\r\n\r\nprint(largest_x)\n```", 'To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nFirst, we can combine the last two congruences since they both have the same remainder:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11 \\cdot 13} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( x = 143k + 10 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Now we need to find \\( k \\) such that:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 143k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. Since \\( 6 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\), the inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6. So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nThis means that \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010, so we solve for the largest \\( m \\) such that:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\]\nSince \\( \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.283 \\), the largest integer \\( m \\) can be is 1. So:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nThus, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given con