, we need to set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's denote the cost of one pencil by \\( p \\) and the cost of a jumbo eraser by \\( e \\). The equations can be written as follows:\n\n1. \\( 3p + e = 1.24 \\)\n2. \\( 5p + e = 1.82 \\)\n\nWe can solve these equations step-by-step. First, we can subtract the first equation from the second to eliminate \\( e \\):\n\n\\[ (5p + e) - (3p + e) = 1.82 - 1.24 \\]\n\\[ 2p = 0.58 \\]\n\\[ p = \\frac{0.58}{2} \\]\n\\[ p = 0.29 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of one pencil is \\( \\$0.29 \\). To convert this to cents, we multiply by 100:\n\n\\[ p = 0.29 \\times 100 = 29 \\text{ cents} \\]\n\nLet's verify this solution by substituting \\( p = 0.29 \\) back into one of the original equations to find the cost of the jumbo eraser. We'll use the first equation:\n\n\\[ 3(0.29) + e = 1.24 \\]\n\\[ 0.87 + e = 1.24 \\]\n\\[ e = 1.24 - 0.87 \\]\n\\[ e = 0.37 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a jumbo eraser is \\( \\$0.37 \\). This solution satisfies both equations, confirming that our answer is correct.\n\nThe cost of a pencil in cents is \\(\\boxed{29}\\).", 'In the addition problem below, each letter represents a different digit. What is the value of $A+B+C+D+E+F$?\n\\[\\begin{array}{cccccc}\nA&B&C&D&E&F\\\\\n+&&&&&F\\\\\n\\hline\nD&E&F&A&B&C\n\\end{array}\\] \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed