1)}{72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\nWe can cancel out the 25 from both sides:\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}} = \\frac{1}{216} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by $72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}$:\n\\[ n(n-1) = \\frac{72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}}{216} \\]\nSimplifying the right side:\n\\[ n(n-1) = \\frac{72 \\cdot 6^{n-2}}{6^3} = \\frac{72}{6^2} = 2 \\cdot 6^{n-4} \\]\nSo we have:\n\\[ n(n-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{n-4} \\]\nWe can now test values of $n$ to find which one satisfies this equation. Let\'s start with $n = 4$:\n\\[ 4(4-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{4-4} \\]\n\\[ 4 \\cdot 3 = 2 \\cdot 6^0 \\]\n\\[ 12 = 2 \\cdot 1 \\]\n\\[ 12 = 2 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 4$. Next, let\'s try $n = 5$:\n\\[ 5(5-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{5-4} \\]\n\\[ 5 \\cdot 4 = 2 \\cdot 6^1 \\]\n\\[ 20 = 2 \\cdot 6 \\]\n\\[ 20 = 12 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 5$. Next, let\'s try $n = 6$:\n\\[ 6(6-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{6-4} \\]\n\\[ 6 \\cdot 5 = 2 \\cdot 6^2 \\]\n\\[ 30 = 2 \\cdot 36 \\]\n\\[ 30 = 72 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 6$. Next, let\'s try $n = 7$:\n\\[ 7(7-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{7-4} \\]\n\\[ 7 \\cdot 6 = 2 \\cdot 6^3 \\]\n\\[ 42 = 2 \\cdot 216 \\]\n\\[ 42 = 432 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 7$. Next, let\'s try $n = 8$:\n\\[ 8(8-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{8-4} \\]\n\\[ 8 \\cdot 7 = 2 \\cdot 6^4 \\]\n\\[ 56 = 2 \\cdot 1296 \\]\n\\[ 56 = 2592 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 8$. Next, let\'s try $n = 9$:\n\\[ 9(9-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{9-4} \\]\n\\[ 9 \\cdot 8 = 2 \\cdot 6^5 \\]\n\\[ 72 = 2 \\cdot 7776 \\]\n\\[ 72 = 15552 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 9$. Next, let\'s try $n = 10$:\n\\[ 10(10-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{10-4} \\]\n\\[ 10 \\cdot 9 = 2 \\cdot 6^6 \\]\n\\[ 90 = 2 \\cdot 46656 \\]\n\\[ 90 = 93312 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 10$. Next, let\'s try $n = 11$:\n\\[ 11(11-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{11-4} \\]\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 10 = 2 \\cdot 6^7 \\]\n\\[ 110 = 2 \\cdot 279936 \\]\n\\[ 110 = 559872 \\]\nThis is not true, so $n \\neq 11$. Next, let\'s try $n = 12$:\n\\[ 12(12-1) = 2 \\cdot 6^{12-4} \\]\n\\[ 12 \\cdot 11 = 2 \