begin{cases} \n6C_p + C_c = 1.30 \\\\\n24C_p + 8C_c = 6.80 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe can solve these equations using Python. Once we have \\(C_p\\) and \\(C_c\\), we can calculate the cost of a 3-inch cube using the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Cost} = 54C_p + 27C_c \\]\n\nLet's proceed with the calculations in Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nCp, Cc = symbols('Cp Cc')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations based on the given information\r\neq1 = Eq(6*Cp + Cc, 1.30)\r\neq2 = Eq(24*Cp + 8*Cc, 6.80)\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (Cp, Cc))\r\nCp_value = solution[Cp]\r\nCc_value = solution[Cc]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the cost of a 3-inch cube\r\ncost_3_inch_cube = 54*Cp_value + 27*Cc_value\r\nprint((Cp_value, Cc_value, cost_3_inch_cube.evalf()))\n```\n```output\n(0.100000000000000, 0.700000000000000, 20.7000000000000)\n```\nThe values we obtained are:\n- \\(C_p = 0.10\\)\n- \\(C_c = 0.70\\)\n\nUsing these values, the cost of a 3-inch cube is:\n\\[ \\text{Cost} = 54 \\cdot 0.10 + 27 \\cdot 0.70 = 5.40 + 18.90 = 20.70 \\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a 3-inch cube is \\(\\boxed{20.70}\\).", 'A 10 foot long ladder is leaning against a wall.  The bottom of the ladder is 4 feet from the wall.  How far up the wall does the ladder reach?  (Round to the nearest hundredth of a foot.)  \\boxed{  \\text{A. } 9.16 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{B. } 9.17 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{C. } 9.18 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{D. } 9.19 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{E. } 9.20 \\text{ ft}  \\quad \\text{F. } 9.21 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{G. } 9.22 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{H. } 9.23 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{I. } 9.24 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{J. } 9.25 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{K. } 9.26 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{L. } 9.27 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{M. } 9.28 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{N. } 9.29 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{O. } 9.30 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{P. } 9.31 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{Q. } 9.32 \\text{ ft} \\quad \\text{R. } 