llel\\overline{QR}$, we know that $\\angle STR$ and $\\angle Q$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, $\\angle STR = \\angle Q = \\boxed{35^\\circ}$.\n\nThe reasoning behind this is that corresponding angles are congruent when two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. In this case, $\\overline{ST}$ and $\\overline{QR}$ are parallel, and $\\overline{PQ}$ is the transversal. Therefore, $\\angle STR$ and $\\angle Q$ are corresponding angles and must be congruent. Since $\\angle Q = 35^\\circ$, we can conclude that $\\angle STR = \\boxed{35^\\circ}$.', "To find the measure of \\(\\angle STR\\), we need to use the properties of parallel lines and the angles they form. Given that \\(\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}\\), the corresponding angles and the alternate interior angles will be equal.\n\nHere's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Since \\(\\overline{ST} \\parallel \\overline{QR}\\), \\(\\angle STR\\) and \\(\\angle QRP\\) are corresponding angles. Therefore, \\(\\angle STR = \\angle QRP\\).\n2. To find \\(\\angle QRP\\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\). In \\(\\triangle PQR\\), we have \\(\\angle P = 40^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle Q = 35^\\circ\\).\n3. The measure of \\(\\angle QRP\\) can be found by subtracting the sum of \\(\\angle P\\) and \\(\\angle Q\\) from \\(180^\\circ\\):\n   \\[\n   \\angle QRP = 180^\\circ - \\angle P - \\angle Q = 180^\\circ - 40^\\circ - 35^\\circ = 105^\\circ.\n   \\]\n4. Since \\(\\angle STR = \\angle QRP\\), we have \\(\\angle STR = 105^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code for accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_P = 40\nangle_Q = 35\n\n# Calculate angle QRP using the fact that the sum of angles in a triangle is 180 degrees\nangle_QRP = 180 - angle_P - angle_Q\n\n# Since angle STR is equal to angle QRP (corresponding angles)\nangle_STR = angle_QRP\n\nprint(angle_STR)\n```\n```output\n105\n```\nThe measure of \\(\\angle STR\\) is \\(\\boxed{105}\\) degrees.