sum}=7) = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\right) + p \\cdot q + q \\cdot p = \\frac{2}{36} + \\frac{2pq}{1} = \\frac{1}{18} + 2pq = \\frac{47}{288}.\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the equation, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1}{18} + 2pq = \\frac{47}{288}.\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2pq = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{1}{18} = \\frac{47}{288} - \\frac{16}{288} = \\frac{31}{288}.\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     pq = \\frac{31}{576}.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solve for $p$ and $q$:**\n   - From the equation $p + q + \\frac{5}{6} = 1$, we get $p + q = \\frac{1}{6}$.\n   - We now have a system of equations:\n     \\[\n     p + q = \\frac{1}{6},\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     pq = \\frac{31}{576}.\n     \\]\n   - These equations represent a quadratic equation in terms of $p$ and $q$: $t^2 - \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)t + \\frac{31}{576} = 0$.\n   - Solving the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula $t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$, we get:\n     \\[\n     t = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^2 - 4 \\cdot \\frac{31}{576}}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} - \\frac{31}{144}}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{144} - \\frac{31}{144}}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-27}{144}}}{2}.\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     t = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{27}}{12}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{12}}{2} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{6} \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}}{2} = \\frac{2 \\pm 3\\sqrt{3}}{24}.\n     \\]\n   - Since $p > \\frac{1}{6}$ and $q < \\frac{1}{6}$, we have:\n     \\[\n     p = \\frac{2 + 3\\sqrt{3}}{24}, \\quad q = \\frac{2 - 3\\sqrt{3}}{24}.\n     \\]\n   - The probability of obtaining face $F$ is $p = \\frac{2 + 3\\sqrt{3}}{24}$.\n\n5. **Simplify the Fraction:**\n   - The fraction $\\frac{2 + 3\\sqrt{3}}{24}$ is already in its simplest form since the numerator and denominator have no common factors other than 1.\n   