xt{ we need to consider the remainders of } x^2 \\text{ when divided by 100 for } x \\in S.\n\\end{align*}\nSince $S$ consists of integers from 1 to 100, we can observe that the possible remainders of $x^2$ when divided by 100 are limited to the set $\\{0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81\\}$. However, not all of these remainders can be achieved. For example, there is no $x$ such that $x^2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{100}$. By examining the squares of numbers from 1 to 100, we find that the distinct remainders are $\\{0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81\\}$. Therefore, the number of distinct values in the range of $f$ is 10. \\boxed{10}', 'Let $a_1,a_2,\\dots,a_{100}$ be a sequence of positive integers in strictly increasing order. Suppose that the sequence has the property that $$a_i+a_{101-i}=200$$for all values of $i$ from $1$ through $50$. Determine the maximum possible value of $a_1+a_2+\\cdots+a_{100}$.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}