 a 90% confidence interval. The z-score is a value that represents the number of standard deviations a given proportion is from the mean. For a 90% confidence interval, the z-score is approximately 1.645.\n\nThe formula for the confidence interval is:\np̂ ± z * sqrt[(p̂ * (1 - p̂)) / n]\n\nwhere p̂ is the sample proportion, z is the z-score, and n is the sample size.\n\nSubstituting the values, we get:\n0.4 ± 1.645 * sqrt[(0.4 * (1 - 0.4)) / 500]\n\nSolving this equation will give us the 90% confidence interval for the percentage of persons aged 18 to 24 in the city who are attending college.\n\nHuman: How can we calculate the area of a triangle using its base and height in a programming context, specifically in MATLAB or Octave?\n\nAssistant: To calculate the area of a triangle given its base `b` and height `h` in MATLAB or Octave, you can use the following function:\n\n```matlab\nfunction area = trg_area(b, h)\n    area = 0.5 * b * h;\nend\n```\n\nThis function takes the base and height as inputs and returns the area of the triangle. The area is calculated using the formula `area = 0.5 * b * h`. This formula is derived from the fact that the area of a triangle is half the product of its base and height.\n\nTo verify the correctness of this function, you can use a test suite with known values for the base and height, and expected area results. For example:\n\n```matlab\n% Test 1\nb = 1; h = 5;\ny_correct = 2.5;\nassert(isequal(trg_area(b, h), y_correct))\n\n% Test 2\nb = 1; h = 10;\ny_correct = 5;\nassert(isequal(trg_area(b, h), y_correct))\n```\n\nThese tests will pass if the `trg_area` function correctly calculates the area of the triangle.\n\nHuman: Given the following data for a sample of 235 customers on the number of hours cars are parked and the amount they are charged in a Downtown Tampa parking lot:\n- Number of Hours: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\n- Frequency: 18, 33, 49, 44, 34, 15, 8, 34\n- Amount Charged: \\$3, \\$7, \\$10, \\$15, \\$19, \\$24, \\$27, \\$30\