istances[(route[i], route[i + 1])]\n    return total_distance\n\n# Calculate the distances for all routes and find the minimum\nmin_distance = float('inf')\nfor route in routes:\n    distance = calculate_distance(route)\n    if distance < min_distance:\n        min_distance = distance\n\nprint(min_distance)\n```\n```output\n17\n```\nThe shortest distance that can be traveled starting at any point \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), or \\(D\\) and visiting the other three points once is \\(\\boxed{17}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the shortest path that visits all four points \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\) exactly once, starting from any of these points. This is a classic example of the Traveling Salesman Problem (TSP) for a complete graph with four vertices.\n\nWe can solve this problem by calculating the total distance for each possible path and then selecting the shortest one. There are \\(4! = 24\\) possible permutations of the points \\(A, B, C, D\\), but since we are starting from any point, we can fix the starting point and only consider the permutations of the remaining three points. This reduces the number of permutations to \\(3! = 6\\).\n\nLet's list all possible paths starting from \\(A\\):\n\n1. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\)\n2. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C\\)\n3. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow D\\)\n4. \\(A \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B\\)\n5. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C\\)\n6. \\(A \\rightarrow D \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow B\\)\n\nWe will calculate the total distance for each path and find the shortest one. The distances between the points are given as follows:\n- \\(AB = 6\\)\n- \\(AC = 6\\)\n- \\(AD = 5\\)\n- \\(BC = 5\\)\n- \\(BD = 3\\)\n- \\(CD = 4\\)\n\nLet's calculate the total distance for each path starting from \\(A\\):\n\n1. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C \\rightarrow D\\): \\(6 + 5 + 4 = 15\\)\n2. \\(A \\rightarrow B \\righta