ality, we find that $x-2 < 100$, or $x < 102$. Additionally, the denominator cannot be zero, which means $2-\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$, or $\\log(x-2) \\neq 1$. This implies $x-2 \\neq 10$, or $x \\neq 12$. Combining these conditions, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(2, 10) \\cup (10, 102)$.', "To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. This involves ensuring that the denominator is not zero and that the argument of the logarithm is positive.\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step:\n\n1. **Argument of the Outer Logarithm:** The argument of the outer logarithm \\(2 - \\log(x-2)\\) must be positive. This gives us the inequality:\n   \\[\n   2 - \\log(x-2) > 0 \\implies \\log(x-2) < 2 \\implies x-2 < 10^2 \\implies x < 102.\n   \\]\n   Additionally, since the argument of the logarithm must be positive, we have:\n   \\[\n   x-2 > 0 \\implies x > 2.\n   \\]\n   So, combining these, we get:\n   \\[\n   2 < x < 102.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Denominator Non-Zero:** The denominator \\(\\log(2 - \\log(x-2))\\) must not be zero. This means:\n   \\[\n   2 - \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies \\log(x-2) \\neq 1 \\implies x-2 \\neq 10^1 \\implies x \\neq 12.\n   \\]\n\nCombining these conditions, the domain of \\( f(x) \\) is all \\( x \\) in the interval \\( (2, 102) \\) except \\( x = 12 \\). In interval notation, this is:\n\\[\n(2, 12) \\cup (12, 102).\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nf = (2 - x) / sp.log(2 - sp.log(x - 2, 10), 10)\n\n# Find the domain\ndomain = sp.calculus.util.continuous_domain(f, x, sp.S.Reals)\nprint(domain)\n```\n```output\nUnion(Interval.open(2, 12), Interval.open(12, 102))\n```\nThe domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2-x}{\\log(2-\\log(x-2))} \\) is indeed \\((2, 12) \\cup (12, 102)\\).\n\nSo, the final answer in interval notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2, 1